1
00:00:29,046 --> 00:00:32,309
[SPEAKER_03]: Maybe I'll start with a simple one.

2
00:00:33,290 --> 00:00:41,777
[SPEAKER_03]: I was reading about the abandonment of the tendencies that we have towards pleasant, unpleasant and neutral feelings.

3
00:00:42,938 --> 00:00:43,939
[SPEAKER_03]: And there was...

4
00:00:44,259 --> 00:00:49,984
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm not totally clear on the tendencies that are supposed to come from the neutral feelings.

5
00:00:50,584 --> 00:00:51,525
[SPEAKER_03]: Specifically, it says

6
00:00:52,461 --> 00:01:08,390
[SPEAKER_03]: When one is touched by neither painful nor pleasant feeling, if one understands, as it actually is, the origination, the disappearance, the gratification, the danger and the escape in regard to that feeling, then the underlying tendency to ignorance does not lie within this one.

7
00:01:09,251 --> 00:01:10,812
[SPEAKER_03]: And this just confuses me.

8
00:01:12,173 --> 00:01:20,497
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't understand one bit of it, except that it fuels ignorance, but I'm not sure how.

9
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:30,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the neutral feeling is the one that's pretty much not seen, generally, by people.

10
00:01:33,614 --> 00:01:37,836
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the feeling that's hardest to see for what it is, as a phenomenon that endures.

11
00:01:38,016 --> 00:01:41,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, it's neither pleasant nor unpleasant.

12
00:01:41,717 --> 00:01:48,039
[SPEAKER_00]: People might have an idea about it, but they cannot actually... You know, it's easy to see when you're feeling bad.

13
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:49,360
[SPEAKER_00]: It's obvious.

14
00:01:50,338 --> 00:01:52,739
[SPEAKER_00]: It's easy to see when you're related with pleasure.

15
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:53,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It's obvious.

16
00:01:54,140 --> 00:01:56,482
[SPEAKER_00]: But what it's not obvious is when it's kind of neither.

17
00:01:57,422 --> 00:02:01,505
[SPEAKER_00]: And because it's not obvious, that's why that is felt unpleasantly.

18
00:02:02,165 --> 00:02:03,046
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't know it.

19
00:02:03,946 --> 00:02:08,569
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when people feel neither, they don't find that peaceful and agreeable.

20
00:02:09,149 --> 00:02:13,692
[SPEAKER_00]: They find that as a kind of, you know, dull, pressuring discomfort.

21
00:02:14,807 --> 00:02:25,094
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's... in the talks on our YouTube channel, I did a few on that, and it is practically... it's like, you know, when you're bored, how it starts.

22
00:02:26,154 --> 00:02:35,941
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't feel bad, but by not feeling good either, and by not having any kind of distraction to take your mind away from it, you start feeling unpleasantly.

23
00:02:36,761 --> 00:02:38,843
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is still a neutral feeling.

24
00:02:40,216 --> 00:02:44,938
[SPEAKER_00]: But because it's not known, it's felt as a form of discomfort.

25
00:02:45,859 --> 00:02:46,619
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that lack of...

26
00:02:46,639 --> 00:02:52,562
[SPEAKER_03]: So, we basically escalated on our own from neutral to, well, unpleasant afterwards.

27
00:02:53,202 --> 00:03:01,006
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sort of, but I wouldn't say escalate, because ignorance is already there with that feeling, because it's not understood.

28
00:03:01,126 --> 00:03:07,929
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no, like, a first point where that feeling is clear, and then you get confused, and then it escalates into pain.

29
00:03:09,442 --> 00:03:14,745
[SPEAKER_00]: You already don't know it, when it is there, which means pain is already there with that lack of knowledge.

30
00:03:15,826 --> 00:03:18,968
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the suttas, maybe even the same suttas, says.

31
00:03:19,488 --> 00:03:22,370
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's not known, it's felt unpleasantly.

32
00:03:22,510 --> 00:03:24,191
[SPEAKER_00]: When it's known, it's felt pleasantly.

33
00:03:24,431 --> 00:03:27,433
[SPEAKER_00]: By the wise, they find, they understand that's where the peace is.

34
00:03:29,056 --> 00:03:30,317
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the ignorance is that.

35
00:03:30,678 --> 00:03:39,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Not knowing that, not understanding, not being able to endure the pressure of the neutral feeling, seeking the distraction out of it.

36
00:03:40,051 --> 00:03:42,594
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is what perpetuates that ignorance.

37
00:03:44,948 --> 00:03:49,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance is not a lack of information or some, you know, metaphysical secret or something.

38
00:03:50,730 --> 00:03:59,692
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not being able to endure the discomfort of a neutral feeling, acting out of it, seeking the distractions, perpetuating it, feeding it.

39
00:03:59,872 --> 00:04:05,093
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the ignorance that underlines it and that you keep sustaining in that very underlining it.

40
00:04:07,654 --> 00:04:11,255
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when you do identify the neutral feeling,

41
00:04:13,185 --> 00:04:24,595
[SPEAKER_03]: So, for example, the first way to see it would be to recognize, let's say, when you're bored or when you're looking for something like, it's not bad, but I could have something more.

42
00:04:25,316 --> 00:04:29,079
[SPEAKER_03]: Would that be a good way to pin it down?

43
00:04:29,319 --> 00:04:30,020
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, yeah.

44
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:34,064
[SPEAKER_00]: When you have nothing to do, catch yourself before you start doing something.

45
00:04:35,005 --> 00:04:37,528
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, like, why is it that you want to do it?

46
00:04:37,568 --> 00:04:38,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is it rooted?

47
00:04:39,229 --> 00:04:45,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is it because you don't want... you're already trying to prevent the boredom, even before it came, so to speak?

48
00:04:46,676 --> 00:04:49,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often people always have something to do lined up.

49
00:04:50,821 --> 00:04:58,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And the moment they sort of run out of things to do and calm down, they're already going for the next thing.

50
00:04:58,691 --> 00:05:08,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is not, you know, often it's seen how I must do this, and this is this is necessary, but more often than not, actually, it's not, or certainly not as necessary.

51
00:05:08,858 --> 00:05:13,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, people have this accumulated pressure.

52
00:05:13,841 --> 00:05:15,262
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, I still must do this.

53
00:05:15,742 --> 00:05:20,486
[SPEAKER_00]: And they confuse that pressure to be like a pressure of duty, but it's really pressure of escaping the boredom.

54
00:05:21,286 --> 00:05:26,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, well, pressure of escaping the fear that comes if you don't distract yourself from boredom.

55
00:05:27,409 --> 00:05:30,651
[SPEAKER_00]: Because boredom, if you sit with it, it's going to become very unpleasant.

56
00:05:31,211 --> 00:05:38,854
[SPEAKER_00]: If you refuse to do anything and just sit with yourself, it's going to result in anxiety, more often than not, for people that haven't been through that.

57
00:05:40,215 --> 00:05:45,337
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why when you kind of relish the activity and finding things to do that you depend on.

58
00:05:48,539 --> 00:05:54,442
[SPEAKER_03]: I remember hearing one of your talks where you challenged us to be bored and endure it.

59
00:05:55,779 --> 00:06:00,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, like, even meditation practice for people becomes a something to do, so I don't get bored.

60
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:05,021
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, thank you.

61
00:06:05,041 --> 00:06:14,743
[SPEAKER_01]: I would actually have a question on meditation, if that's okay.

62
00:06:16,564 --> 00:06:20,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Namely, the mindfulness of breathing meditation.

63
00:06:21,445 --> 00:06:23,005
[SPEAKER_01]: This seems to be the most

64
00:06:23,363 --> 00:06:32,087
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a commonly prescribed method of meditation in various dhamma talks by various teachers, very popular and widespread.

65
00:06:34,468 --> 00:06:49,956
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually, this instruction in these dhamma talks stops at the feeling the breath coming in and out of your body, maybe feeling the entire body.

66
00:06:50,696 --> 00:06:52,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Some say feeling the body of the breath,

67
00:06:53,686 --> 00:07:05,135
[SPEAKER_01]: But, reading the suttas, you can see that mindfulness of breathing was much more elaborately described, or this instruction was much more elaborate.

68
00:07:06,056 --> 00:07:16,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are such additional units to this instruction, such as, you know, tranquilizing the body, experiencing rapture, experiencing pleasure.

69
00:07:17,624 --> 00:07:22,108
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, I'm also wondering about this experiencing the mental formations.

70
00:07:23,197 --> 00:07:47,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Experiencing the entire mind, and then gladdening, concentrating, and liberating the mind, and also contemplating impermanence, fading away, cessation, liberation, liberation, as well as contemplating the elements of the elements, so the four elements plus space, and kindness, and so the brahmanikāras.

71
00:07:47,496 --> 00:07:49,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I was wondering,

72
00:07:51,339 --> 00:07:59,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think... was this meant to be like a step-by-step instruction on what to do in one sitting?

73
00:08:00,822 --> 00:08:17,947
[SPEAKER_01]: And in that case, I don't think I remember all the motions when I have to... when I sit down and... Or was it just, you know, just general guidelines on what kind of sentiments or, I don't know, dispositions to

74
00:08:19,183 --> 00:08:22,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, first you need to become a sotāpanna, as the sutta itself says.

75
00:08:22,745 --> 00:08:27,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the something that everybody seems to be overlooking and ignoring.

76
00:08:49,828 --> 00:08:57,070
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha told ānāpānasati to the monks that were very much already on the path and they haven't quite completed it.

77
00:08:57,130 --> 00:08:59,890
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he said, oh, well, let me teach those who still have work to do.

78
00:09:01,390 --> 00:09:06,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it does not apply to people that can't even have the sign of their mind and have the right view.

79
00:09:07,012 --> 00:09:08,392
[SPEAKER_00]: It simply doesn't apply.

80
00:09:08,412 --> 00:09:19,234
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be misunderstood, misperformed, or as that other sutra says, mismeditated, undermeditated, overmeditated, premeditated, everything else, but not meditated.

81
00:09:20,665 --> 00:09:23,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So really, that should be the concern.

82
00:09:23,447 --> 00:09:24,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting the right view.

83
00:09:24,768 --> 00:09:30,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, what samādhi is, that will be known through that right view.

84
00:09:30,192 --> 00:09:36,937
[SPEAKER_00]: But just to answer your theoretical aspect of ānāpānasati, no, it's not meant to be done as a step-by-step.

85
00:09:37,077 --> 00:09:38,839
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like the most comprehensive

86
00:09:39,588 --> 00:09:52,495
[SPEAKER_00]: approach to the practice of mindfulness, on account of an pretty much inherent, inseparable bodily activity, in every possible aspect, they will absolutely remove any traces of ignorance left.

87
00:09:53,435 --> 00:09:59,939
[SPEAKER_00]: But more often than not, monks were just, you know, doing it in that, in the broadest sense, how every description starts.

88
00:10:00,119 --> 00:10:02,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowingly he breathes in, knowingly he breathes out.

89
00:10:03,793 --> 00:10:12,216
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was another sutra where a monk said, oh, that's how he practices it, and he abandons sensuality of the past, sensuality of the future, and sensuality of the present.

90
00:10:12,276 --> 00:10:19,238
[SPEAKER_00]: He has no more sensual desires, because he has fully developed mindfulness of breathing on the level of knowingly breathe in, knowingly breathe out.

91
00:10:19,818 --> 00:10:25,500
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha said, yeah, that's fine, but you should do even more, and then goes into those other steps.

92
00:10:26,491 --> 00:10:35,979
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, that monk still had work to do, on a subtle level, which he wouldn't have completed if he didn't, obviously, refine further that mindfulness of breathing.

93
00:10:42,565 --> 00:10:47,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, because it continues with the entering the jhānas, and it's a...

94
00:10:48,751 --> 00:10:50,993
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, see, that's the whole, like...

95
00:10:52,758 --> 00:11:18,419
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that follows on its own, as a result of the right view, which means it would be closer to the right ānāpānasati, say, for somebody without a right view, it will be closer for them to do the meditation, mindfulness of breathing correctly, not by watching their nostrils or breathing or following the steps, but by actually seeing the reason behind them wanting to do it in the first place.

96
00:11:19,728 --> 00:11:22,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I want to do this particular mindfulness of breathing?

97
00:11:23,669 --> 00:11:24,670
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I hoping for?

98
00:11:25,270 --> 00:11:26,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Where are my motivations rooted?

99
00:11:26,630 --> 00:11:27,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they're still there.

100
00:11:27,611 --> 00:11:28,611
[SPEAKER_00]: They will be enduring there.

101
00:11:28,871 --> 00:11:32,192
[SPEAKER_00]: They will be the reasons for your action, the reason for your effort.

102
00:11:33,053 --> 00:11:36,054
[SPEAKER_00]: So, are you trying to gain something as a result of it?

103
00:11:36,534 --> 00:11:40,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Or are you trying to undo that which already prevents you from having the right view?

104
00:11:42,556 --> 00:11:44,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Because right view is not gained.

105
00:11:44,477 --> 00:11:45,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Right view is uncovered.

106
00:11:47,072 --> 00:11:50,215
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a completely opposite direction of where people usually go for.

107
00:11:51,876 --> 00:11:55,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it goes against the stream, against the grain.

108
00:11:56,160 --> 00:12:01,004
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much the nature of that, knowing the motivation behind your action.

109
00:12:01,104 --> 00:12:06,348
[SPEAKER_00]: It's behind the grain of your action, behind the direction where you're heading to.

110
00:12:06,588 --> 00:12:12,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever meditation you want to be doing, it's more important to know why and what for.

111
00:12:14,170 --> 00:12:22,054
[SPEAKER_00]: And don't just take it for granted as something that I must do, because Buddhists meditate, and whatever comes out of it, it will be good for me.

112
00:12:23,655 --> 00:12:31,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, as many Suttas say, more often than not, people like that would end up meditating with their own sensuality.

113
00:12:33,099 --> 00:12:35,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means no good will come out of it.

114
00:12:36,821 --> 00:12:40,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And that whole notion of what concentration in itself is good,

115
00:12:43,485 --> 00:12:45,906
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a very relative statement.

116
00:12:46,526 --> 00:12:48,487
[SPEAKER_00]: Concentration itself is neither good nor bad.

117
00:12:48,507 --> 00:12:50,708
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just a state of mind directed upon something.

118
00:12:51,849 --> 00:12:58,451
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the motivation behind that directing that what determines your intention, basically, as we spoke in the last Sunday.

119
00:12:59,152 --> 00:13:04,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Your intention determines whether, you know, what you're about to do is rooted in... is good or not.

120
00:13:04,974 --> 00:13:06,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it rooted in desire, aversion and so on?

121
00:13:09,753 --> 00:13:21,091
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole point is to recognize that, where the problem is, that it's not in what you're doing as much in terms of meditation and what technique and how to go about it.

122
00:13:22,011 --> 00:13:26,634
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really on the level as to what for and what you're hoping to achieve with it.

123
00:13:26,654 --> 00:13:27,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you clear?

124
00:13:27,514 --> 00:13:30,476
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, do you know exactly what this meditation is supposed to result in?

125
00:13:31,096 --> 00:13:40,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you're just doing it and then you're basically hoping for some agreeable experiences that will be of the spiritual kind.

126
00:13:40,602 --> 00:13:42,323
[SPEAKER_00]: Means there is no knowledge to begin with.

127
00:13:42,383 --> 00:13:42,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

128
00:13:42,883 --> 00:13:45,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're just chasing the novelties.

129
00:13:45,705 --> 00:13:48,747
[SPEAKER_00]: No, not you specifically, just in general, who I was doing it.

130
00:13:51,823 --> 00:13:53,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

131
00:13:54,105 --> 00:14:06,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, I was reading Sutta number 64, Mahāmāluṅkā, Mahāmāluṅkā Sutta.

132
00:14:07,595 --> 00:14:15,301
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's a brilliant synthesis of everything also that you just said.

133
00:14:15,701 --> 00:14:20,465
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, because it distinguishes between, so the Buddha distinguishes between

134
00:14:21,429 --> 00:14:31,417
[SPEAKER_01]: You have the tūjana there, you have the ordinary person, and then you have someone who has been trained, so, I don't know, ārya.

135
00:14:32,878 --> 00:14:36,140
[SPEAKER_01]: And, the way he...

136
00:14:36,341 --> 00:14:47,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the thing is, the question is how to escape, for example, so how to escape from identity view, so the five lower factors among these, also the identity view, and it's...

137
00:14:49,210 --> 00:15:02,012
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, when you teach the dhamma to someone for the cessation of personality view, then their mind must really enter into this.

138
00:15:02,792 --> 00:15:08,853
[SPEAKER_01]: And the first precondition, if I understood this correctly, is what you've been saying all along.

139
00:15:08,973 --> 00:15:11,894
[SPEAKER_01]: This is seclusion from sensual pleasures.

140
00:15:12,134 --> 00:15:15,675
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the precondition for them to even... Yeah.

141
00:15:15,715 --> 00:15:17,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual pleasure in unwholesome states.

142
00:15:17,535 --> 00:15:18,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, absolutely.

143
00:15:18,995 --> 00:15:19,455
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

144
00:15:20,275 --> 00:15:20,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

145
00:15:21,035 --> 00:15:34,278
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I like how, you know, the Buddha goes on to explain that whatever exists in form, feeling, perception, formations and consciousness, which is basically everything there is, right?

146
00:15:34,318 --> 00:15:36,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything that we can see, experience.

147
00:15:37,238 --> 00:15:37,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

148
00:15:37,958 --> 00:15:47,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything you must see as impermanent, as causing suffering or unsatisfaction, and end not.

149
00:15:48,291 --> 00:15:56,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, and the only reason you need to see these things as causing suffering is not just like to torture yourself or cause pain to yourself.

150
00:15:56,738 --> 00:16:02,082
[SPEAKER_00]: It's only because, by default, they're seen as agreeable and friendly, while they're not.

151
00:16:04,304 --> 00:16:14,732
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are other suttas where the Buddha compares, like, you know, your own mind to, like, basically, like an assassin that pretends to be a friend, but just really wants to kill you.

152
00:16:15,817 --> 00:16:20,000
[SPEAKER_00]: See, the danger is not in much in effect that an assassin wants to kill you.

153
00:16:20,180 --> 00:16:22,661
[SPEAKER_00]: The danger is in you thinking he's your friend.

154
00:16:24,302 --> 00:16:28,465
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stop thinking that he's your friend, he can't surprise you.

155
00:16:28,525 --> 00:16:32,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, doesn't matter how much of an assassin he is, you always see where he is.

156
00:16:33,328 --> 00:16:37,950
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the problem is not in the mind wanting to kill you or the aggregates wanting to cause you suffering.

157
00:16:38,030 --> 00:16:45,295
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is in you not seeing that because you keep holding the view that they're friendly, that they're agreeable, that they are yours.

158
00:16:46,496 --> 00:16:47,076
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, yours...

159
00:16:47,296 --> 00:16:47,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

160
00:16:47,816 --> 00:16:49,677
[SPEAKER_01]: This is difficult to say.

161
00:16:49,697 --> 00:16:52,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so you can see... Well, yours...

162
00:16:52,678 --> 00:16:54,898
[SPEAKER_00]: They're seen as yours, not as a choice.

163
00:16:55,399 --> 00:16:59,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing them as yours is the result of seeing them as friendly and non-dangerous.

164
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:02,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, automatically, they're taken as mine.

165
00:17:04,401 --> 00:17:09,723
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you contemplate danger, just to the extent of not taking them as mine.

166
00:17:09,963 --> 00:17:11,343
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you let the assassin be.

167
00:17:11,723 --> 00:17:13,724
[SPEAKER_00]: And he can't surprise you, and you're fine.

168
00:17:16,365 --> 00:17:42,786
[SPEAKER_01]: So, would you say that by seeing how uncomfortable this all, the discomfort that this causes, all of these forms, feelings, perceptions, so, how much suffering there is in those aggregates, just by that, you can see that if it was really, if it was truly yours, you would make sure that it would be pleasant, at least,

169
00:17:43,142 --> 00:17:58,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, you can, obviously, you have to keep contemplating that, you have to keep reminding yourself of that, but before all of that, your actions must already be aligned up with that principle of, oh, this is not mine, this is not worth it.

170
00:17:59,688 --> 00:18:07,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Because often people would not have sufficient amount of sense restraint and guarding of the sense doors, and then they try to contemplate this on top of it.

171
00:18:07,971 --> 00:18:10,672
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's why it cannot take the root, or not as much.

172
00:18:11,610 --> 00:18:17,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Because their own actions are contradicting what they are doing, what they are contemplating.

173
00:18:17,055 --> 00:18:20,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, precepts, sense restraint and guarding the sense doors.

174
00:18:20,178 --> 00:18:24,341
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the prerequisite that really matches what you're contemplating later.

175
00:18:25,262 --> 00:18:26,483
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to have it before.

176
00:18:30,947 --> 00:18:32,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

177
00:18:33,990 --> 00:18:35,071
[SPEAKER_01]: What would you say about this?

178
00:18:35,431 --> 00:18:36,632
[SPEAKER_01]: So, turning away...

179
00:18:38,009 --> 00:18:41,591
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha says, turn your mind away from these states.

180
00:18:43,011 --> 00:18:47,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Turn them away, but then the next question is, what do I turn them towards?

181
00:18:47,873 --> 00:18:49,934
[SPEAKER_01]: So, where do I go then?

182
00:18:49,954 --> 00:18:51,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Where should I turn my mind?

183
00:18:52,195 --> 00:18:56,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, you don't turn it towards... you don't turn it towards a new thing.

184
00:18:56,737 --> 00:19:04,080
[SPEAKER_00]: You just turn it, as I said, to the extent... to the extent that you were turned towards this as friendly and acceptable,

185
00:19:05,343 --> 00:19:09,486
[SPEAKER_00]: You turn away from that friendliness and you don't accept it.

186
00:19:09,866 --> 00:19:14,950
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't acquire a new thing now, because that will become your sense of self again.

187
00:19:16,612 --> 00:19:25,338
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you turn away to the extent that you will turn towards it, but you don't turn away to find something new, because then your mind will become established upon that.

188
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:29,621
[SPEAKER_01]: So, pull your attention back.

189
00:19:31,243 --> 00:19:34,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, you pull your attention back and you'll be attending that.

190
00:19:39,161 --> 00:19:39,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

191
00:19:39,941 --> 00:19:41,702
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to become another thing you're looking at.

192
00:19:43,123 --> 00:19:44,764
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, your own question implies that.

193
00:19:45,464 --> 00:19:47,045
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I turn it towards?

194
00:19:47,285 --> 00:19:49,666
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, there must be something I must attend to.

195
00:19:52,048 --> 00:19:55,409
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you disperse that spotlight of attention?

196
00:19:55,429 --> 00:20:00,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can't disperse the attention because that's part of the aggregates, but you can know the context of it.

197
00:20:03,334 --> 00:20:09,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but you don't attend it, you don't make it into another object, or when you do, you at least don't lose the context of that.

198
00:20:11,098 --> 00:20:12,739
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, that's on the level of the knowledge.

199
00:20:13,479 --> 00:20:26,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, things that you know, even in mundane terms, like, whatever, you know, your knowledge, basic knowledge that you have in regard to things, you don't need to constantly be attending that on a day-to-day basis in order to remember it.

200
00:20:27,347 --> 00:20:27,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Not at all.

201
00:20:27,987 --> 00:20:28,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

202
00:20:29,128 --> 00:20:31,169
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to attend things when you don't know them.

203
00:20:33,403 --> 00:20:44,577
[SPEAKER_00]: When you actually get to understand them, you stop thinking about it, you stop attending it, and when that knowledge is needed, it comes back, because that's what understanding is.

204
00:20:44,637 --> 00:20:47,601
[SPEAKER_00]: And it goes the same for the dhamma practice.

205
00:20:47,741 --> 00:20:52,387
[SPEAKER_00]: When that man asked the Buddha, oh, does the Tathāgata, does he prepare these answers?

206
00:20:53,307 --> 00:20:55,348
[SPEAKER_00]: to the various questions that people ask him.

207
00:20:55,769 --> 00:20:58,451
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, well, let me ask you in return and that will give you the answer.

208
00:20:59,011 --> 00:21:01,793
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the master charioteer and making of the chariots.

209
00:21:02,413 --> 00:21:07,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you, if somebody comes and asks you about various parts of the chariots, do you need to prepare the answer?

210
00:21:08,095 --> 00:21:09,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you know it right away?

211
00:21:09,296 --> 00:21:10,536
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, oh, I know it right away.

212
00:21:10,556 --> 00:21:16,639
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody were to wake me up in the middle of the night, I will know the answer anyway, because I am the master of charity.

213
00:21:16,659 --> 00:21:19,980
[SPEAKER_00]: I fully understood the principles behind how it works, how it's made.

214
00:21:20,501 --> 00:21:22,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha said, so is the same for Tathāgata.

215
00:21:22,562 --> 00:21:25,303
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he doesn't need to prepare the answer, because he fully understood it.

216
00:21:27,604 --> 00:21:34,747
[SPEAKER_00]: So, knowledge, really, just knowledge of the universal characteristics of your experience, cannot be undone and cannot be forgotten.

217
00:21:34,867 --> 00:21:36,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it results in liberation.

218
00:21:37,147 --> 00:21:41,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Not perfect attention, 100%, undivided, never kind of sways.

219
00:21:42,629 --> 00:21:46,991
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's the basically unenlightened mind idea of what enlightenment is.

220
00:21:47,671 --> 00:21:51,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Full absorption into something that's outside of me, that will save me, basically.

221
00:21:54,473 --> 00:22:00,336
[SPEAKER_01]: So, actually, it's the same thing in the instruction of meditation that I was asking about before.

222
00:22:01,316 --> 00:22:04,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the Buddha also says all breaths are known,

223
00:22:04,539 --> 00:22:09,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, knowingly he breathes in, knowingly he breathes out.

224
00:22:09,621 --> 00:22:14,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Not he breathes in feeling his nostrils or sensations in his chest.

225
00:22:15,762 --> 00:22:17,583
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you know you're already breathing.

226
00:22:18,023 --> 00:22:19,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you need more?

227
00:22:19,504 --> 00:22:22,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that knowledge not enough to your mind?

228
00:22:23,065 --> 00:22:24,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because of your wrong assumptions.

229
00:22:26,626 --> 00:22:30,607
[SPEAKER_00]: So, really, the training of the mind is to recognize what it already knows,

230
00:22:31,880 --> 00:22:34,082
[SPEAKER_00]: And realize that it's enough.

231
00:22:34,382 --> 00:22:37,304
[SPEAKER_00]: That knowledge is enough if you sustain it and protect it.

232
00:22:40,206 --> 00:22:49,213
[SPEAKER_00]: And the training of the mind is not basically to sort of develop experiences on its own terms, but what the mind deems agreeable and so on.

233
00:22:49,494 --> 00:22:57,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what the mind deems agreeable is sensuality of one kind or the other.

234
00:22:57,740 --> 00:22:58,661
And this knowledge

235
00:23:00,210 --> 00:23:09,877
[SPEAKER_01]: During meditation or during whatever activity, this knowledge also will self... you know, all the other steps will self-generate.

236
00:23:09,897 --> 00:23:11,738
The rapture will self-generate.

237
00:23:11,838 --> 00:23:13,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure will be there.

238
00:23:13,499 --> 00:23:13,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

239
00:23:13,799 --> 00:23:21,024
[SPEAKER_00]: If you sustain it sufficiently enough, develop your mind, becomes imperturbable, then yes, everything else will follow.

240
00:23:21,044 --> 00:23:21,885
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I said before.

241
00:23:22,805 --> 00:23:23,786
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you said before.

242
00:23:23,886 --> 00:23:28,429
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, yeah, these two sutas are very much interconnected.

243
00:23:29,705 --> 00:23:38,932
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it really comes down to sort of curbing the tendency to follow the steps and find the next steps at the expense of that which you already know.

244
00:23:39,748 --> 00:23:44,731
[SPEAKER_00]: See, everybody who sits down to practice meditational breathing knows that they are already breathing.

245
00:23:45,451 --> 00:23:46,832
[SPEAKER_00]: But that ceases to be enough.

246
00:23:46,852 --> 00:23:50,053
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind stops noticing it and wants something more palpable.

247
00:23:50,734 --> 00:23:52,995
[SPEAKER_00]: I want the feeling of lightness.

248
00:23:53,095 --> 00:23:54,776
[SPEAKER_00]: I want nostrils to feel.

249
00:23:54,796 --> 00:23:58,758
[SPEAKER_00]: I want the lungs... Why is the knowledge not enough?

250
00:23:59,298 --> 00:24:03,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is the knowledge so abstract when it's actually quite factual?

251
00:24:04,213 --> 00:24:07,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind is not trained to recognize that level for what it is.

252
00:24:08,518 --> 00:24:14,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind is dependent on more palpable things, because mind has been acting towards the sense objects its own life.

253
00:24:15,266 --> 00:24:18,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the measure of its existence, the senses.

254
00:24:19,165 --> 00:24:26,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly why even the basic knowledge, even as an instruction, it's not that people... No, but tell me... Tell me the steps.

255
00:24:26,527 --> 00:24:36,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Tell me what I observe and what motions I do, because anything subtler than that is felt like an abstract theory, yet they know that they're breathing at any given time.

256
00:24:37,869 --> 00:24:39,410
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing more factual than that.

257
00:24:51,782 --> 00:25:02,065
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the peaceful, this is the sublime, and this is what you actually, in fact, then turn away, turn your mind away from and towards.

258
00:25:02,946 --> 00:25:04,746
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what you turn... Yeah, you can say that.

259
00:25:04,826 --> 00:25:11,608
[SPEAKER_00]: That knowledge will basically turn your mind away from things that it shouldn't have been... well, it shouldn't have been there in the first place.

260
00:25:11,888 --> 00:25:12,229
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

261
00:25:13,309 --> 00:25:15,330
[SPEAKER_00]: That knowledge will prevent you from

262
00:25:17,562 --> 00:25:22,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Regarding those things as friendly, agreeable and as yours.

263
00:25:23,367 --> 00:25:25,969
[SPEAKER_01]: And I find this very useful.

264
00:25:26,089 --> 00:25:29,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Buddha says, be aware that space is infinite.

265
00:25:29,912 --> 00:25:34,836
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're aware that space is infinite, there's not much place for thoughts there.

266
00:25:35,457 --> 00:25:39,840
[SPEAKER_01]: There's not much place for perceptions, formations, consciousness, anything, anywhere.

267
00:25:40,200 --> 00:25:43,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like the mind completely expands.

268
00:25:44,124 --> 00:25:45,725
[SPEAKER_01]: He's talking about the deathless.

269
00:25:46,309 --> 00:25:46,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

270
00:25:46,629 --> 00:25:50,113
[SPEAKER_01]: He said that nothingness is something.

271
00:26:05,629 --> 00:26:06,950
[SPEAKER_00]: To that extent.

272
00:26:07,050 --> 00:26:07,871
[SPEAKER_00]: It is a phenomenon.

273
00:26:08,351 --> 00:26:16,258
[SPEAKER_00]: The absence of certain things, so it's nothing, it's emptiness and all these negative sort of phenomena are positive in themselves for what they are.

274
00:26:18,519 --> 00:26:20,661
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a mystical experience, it's just factual.

275
00:26:20,881 --> 00:26:24,164
[SPEAKER_00]: Empty of lust, empty of aversion, empty of delusion.

276
00:26:31,006 --> 00:26:37,530
[SPEAKER_03]: Before we continue to the next topic, I just wanted to check if I understood something correctly.

277
00:26:37,950 --> 00:26:41,432
[SPEAKER_03]: So, when I was reading, I don't know, was it the same sutra or a different one?

278
00:26:41,472 --> 00:26:42,633
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know.

279
00:26:42,713 --> 00:26:52,580
[SPEAKER_03]: But there was... it said, as they're breathing in and breathing out, they think about different things.

280
00:26:52,740 --> 00:26:56,182
[SPEAKER_03]: And the way I saw it, the breath was just a trigger.

281
00:26:56,682 --> 00:26:58,163
[SPEAKER_03]: It's a reference point.

282
00:26:58,343 --> 00:26:58,964
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like an anchor.

283
00:26:58,984 --> 00:27:02,567
[SPEAKER_03]: So, ānāpānasati is the same as satipaṭṭhāna.

284
00:27:22,159 --> 00:27:32,482
[SPEAKER_00]: The breathing is always there, as I said in other talks, underneath your thoughts, your feelings, everything else you're concerned about or related about.

285
00:27:32,962 --> 00:27:35,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Underneath all of that, this body is still breathing there.

286
00:27:36,383 --> 00:27:45,505
[SPEAKER_00]: So, knowledge of that is what anchors you from being too driven, too far away in those thoughts and forgetting where the anchor is.

287
00:27:48,707 --> 00:27:55,273
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not so much about wondering how you're breathing and how it feels like, it's just... No, no, no, not at all.

288
00:27:55,334 --> 00:27:57,215
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's knowing that you're breathing.

289
00:27:58,276 --> 00:28:00,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowingly it breathes in, knowingly it breathes out.

290
00:28:01,720 --> 00:28:10,247
[SPEAKER_00]: If that's fully developed, to the extent that it can be developed, all those other steps will become apparent and follow out of that.

291
00:28:10,788 --> 00:28:12,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, I got my answer.

292
00:28:12,289 --> 00:28:12,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

293
00:28:14,961 --> 00:28:16,102
[SPEAKER_01]: We have a question here.

294
00:28:16,222 --> 00:28:22,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you please say something about the role of faith in dhamma practice?

295
00:28:23,165 --> 00:28:23,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

296
00:28:23,545 --> 00:28:35,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So, faith in dhamma practice is, as I said many times before, it's like faith you need to have in a doctor, that you think he might be able to help you.

297
00:28:36,750 --> 00:28:40,932
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have absolutely no faith in what the doctor says,

298
00:28:41,918 --> 00:28:44,900
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't even try the medicine that he prescribes.

299
00:28:46,982 --> 00:28:56,508
[SPEAKER_00]: So... but equally, if the medicine is not working, you don't need to have faith in that doctor, because you saw that his instruction didn't work.

300
00:28:57,269 --> 00:28:58,590
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when you change the doctor.

301
00:28:59,130 --> 00:29:05,275
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a blind faith that you must remain faith... full of faith towards the end... like towards... for the rest of your life.

302
00:29:05,975 --> 00:29:07,897
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the faith that you can be helped,

303
00:29:08,677 --> 00:29:19,283
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of the dhamma practice, and that faith should then be practiced out of, and eventually replaced with the knowledge that, yes, this medicine worked, and I'm healed.

304
00:29:19,684 --> 00:29:26,327
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to have a faith in this doctor anymore, because his medicine worked, and now I'm confident that it was the right instruction.

305
00:29:26,968 --> 00:29:33,612
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why in the sutta, the person who gets the right view, their faith gets replaced as, how do we call it, confirmed confidence.

306
00:29:34,730 --> 00:29:40,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, they know for themselves that the medicine works because they can see themselves healing.

307
00:29:45,155 --> 00:29:53,419
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's certainly not the faith that would be on the level of, oh, I have faith the Buddha was the greatest and I'll be saved on account of that faith.

308
00:29:53,779 --> 00:29:56,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, what in Christianity is often found.

309
00:29:57,441 --> 00:29:59,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind you, in Buddhism, it's often found.

310
00:30:01,603 --> 00:30:03,644
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the faith that should make you practice.

311
00:30:05,450 --> 00:30:14,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, we have another question.

312
00:30:15,820 --> 00:30:24,028
[SPEAKER_01]: This one is, what exactly is right view and how do you get to the right view?

313
00:30:26,365 --> 00:30:40,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Keeping the precepts, seeing the danger in the slightest fault, guarding the sense doors, being moderate in eating, avoiding company, living in seclusion, and that will make you notice your mind for what it is.

314
00:30:41,396 --> 00:30:45,879
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you do, it won't be too hard to see it as something that doesn't belong to you.

315
00:30:48,340 --> 00:30:49,361
[SPEAKER_00]: And that would be the right view.

316
00:30:53,704 --> 00:30:56,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, impermanent suffering and non-self.

317
00:30:57,160 --> 00:31:05,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, people think I must see that, and having not fulfilled all the requirements before that, they will not gonna see it.

318
00:31:06,207 --> 00:31:11,211
[SPEAKER_00]: If they have fulfilled all the requirements before that, they will see it, even if they don't make much effort to see it.

319
00:31:19,978 --> 00:31:25,803
[SPEAKER_03]: I was reading a little bit about non-self as well, and

320
00:31:26,908 --> 00:31:36,815
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, some things are very clear, like all the things you should not consider as self, but then that leaves us with the question, what remains?

321
00:31:37,436 --> 00:31:51,366
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if we take all of this away, the guidelines, the criteria that were stated, were something that is permanent and something that we have control over.

322
00:31:52,487 --> 00:31:56,010
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what in your opinion would be then

323
00:31:56,770 --> 00:31:58,151
[SPEAKER_03]: qualified to be called self.

324
00:31:59,771 --> 00:32:00,671
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't quite understand.

325
00:32:01,172 --> 00:32:03,633
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is permanent and what do you have control over?

326
00:32:04,713 --> 00:32:15,377
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in one of the suttas, it said, so, if things are impermanent, they cause suffering and therefore should not be considered as a self or mind.

327
00:32:15,757 --> 00:32:15,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

328
00:32:15,977 --> 00:32:22,039
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, similarly, it said, if things were mine, then I would have control over them.

329
00:32:22,239 --> 00:32:22,359
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

330
00:32:22,420 --> 00:32:25,681
[SPEAKER_03]: And since I don't have control over them, they're not mine.

331
00:32:26,121 --> 00:32:26,341
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

332
00:32:28,013 --> 00:32:31,715
[SPEAKER_03]: Many different suttas say a lot about all the things that are not self.

333
00:32:32,115 --> 00:32:32,316
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

334
00:32:32,356 --> 00:32:34,237
[SPEAKER_03]: I was wondering, what remains?

335
00:32:35,718 --> 00:32:36,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

336
00:32:37,339 --> 00:32:38,500
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

337
00:32:39,200 --> 00:32:42,302
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing worth being calling self, nothing worth owning.

338
00:32:43,203 --> 00:32:46,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you do, to that extent, you will be liable to suffering.

339
00:32:47,126 --> 00:32:48,827
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole nothingness practice.

340
00:32:51,128 --> 00:32:56,632
[SPEAKER_03]: But, for example, if I think who I am,

341
00:32:57,515 --> 00:33:04,322
[SPEAKER_03]: I think about all the choices I made in my life that led me to this point.

342
00:33:04,382 --> 00:33:10,147
[SPEAKER_03]: So, at any point in life, I could have made a different choice and I would have become a different person.

343
00:33:10,828 --> 00:33:16,853
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if I consider these choices, all the past choices, don't they fill this criteria?

344
00:33:17,073 --> 00:33:17,934
[SPEAKER_03]: I mean, they're permanent.

345
00:33:18,054 --> 00:33:19,115
[SPEAKER_03]: Once they're done, they're done.

346
00:33:19,916 --> 00:33:20,857
[SPEAKER_03]: You cannot change them.

347
00:33:21,657 --> 00:33:23,639
[SPEAKER_03]: And you have control over them.

348
00:33:23,759 --> 00:33:26,921
[SPEAKER_03]: You might not have the control about the circumstances you are in.

349
00:33:26,981 --> 00:33:28,542
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you have a degree of control.

350
00:33:29,023 --> 00:33:29,983
[SPEAKER_00]: But did you have a choice?

351
00:33:30,023 --> 00:33:33,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you have choice in the options that were presented to you, that you chose from?

352
00:33:34,647 --> 00:33:34,827
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

353
00:33:35,187 --> 00:33:35,607
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly.

354
00:33:35,687 --> 00:33:37,469
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the circumstance, I would say.

355
00:33:37,589 --> 00:33:37,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

356
00:33:38,109 --> 00:33:41,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your choice of control is always second to the circumstance.

357
00:33:43,633 --> 00:33:48,337
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it can never be the control of ownership, because it's always within that which you cannot own.

358
00:33:49,124 --> 00:33:54,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the choice you have, it's like a choice of driving a car down the cliff.

359
00:33:55,048 --> 00:33:58,370
[SPEAKER_00]: You can choose to turn left and choose to turn right, but you can't stop it.

360
00:34:00,151 --> 00:34:12,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nobody denies that there is a phenomenon of control and choice, but you want to include that in that which is not liable to your choice, which cannot be penetrated by your choice and your control.

361
00:34:13,272 --> 00:34:15,233
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly why I asked you for the options.

362
00:34:15,713 --> 00:34:22,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, you chose this on account of this, but did you have a say in what arose for you as the options of choosing?

363
00:34:22,635 --> 00:34:22,775
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

364
00:34:23,355 --> 00:34:24,096
[SPEAKER_00]: And you never will.

365
00:34:25,616 --> 00:34:31,098
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even the permanence of the choice is only relatively within the impermanence of the options.

366
00:34:35,700 --> 00:34:36,320
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so...

367
00:34:38,982 --> 00:34:49,745
[SPEAKER_03]: It would be foolish to say that anyone has full control over it, but couldn't we define ourselves relative to the situation?

368
00:34:51,006 --> 00:34:54,166
[SPEAKER_03]: Or would that cause suffering?

369
00:34:54,186 --> 00:34:55,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can, sure.

370
00:34:55,207 --> 00:34:55,827
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, you can.

371
00:34:55,887 --> 00:35:01,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you can define yourself in a relative way, but that's not the problem, nor the point.

372
00:35:01,849 --> 00:35:06,270
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is that any form of definition will kind of

373
00:35:06,955 --> 00:35:13,236
[SPEAKER_00]: Feed the assumption that, to the extent that there is a control, to that extent your sense of self is justified.

374
00:35:13,776 --> 00:35:18,597
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, if you see it correctly as a relative thing, like, sense of self is not seen as a relative.

375
00:35:19,237 --> 00:35:28,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you talk about it in theory as a relatively arisen or formed, fundamentally, sense of self is the owner and the master of this experience.

376
00:35:28,899 --> 00:35:31,400
[SPEAKER_00]: But what kind of master is a relative master?

377
00:35:32,172 --> 00:35:36,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Only a deluded master would say, I'm a master, ignoring his own relativity.

378
00:35:36,934 --> 00:35:39,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if he is relative, he is not in control.

379
00:35:40,675 --> 00:35:41,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, he is not the master.

380
00:35:42,135 --> 00:35:43,136
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all you need to see.

381
00:35:43,636 --> 00:35:46,457
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to destroy that sense of relative self.

382
00:35:47,597 --> 00:35:48,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Or deny it.

383
00:35:48,817 --> 00:35:50,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Or find something greater.

384
00:35:50,278 --> 00:35:52,159
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to disown it completely.

385
00:35:52,619 --> 00:35:53,199
[SPEAKER_00]: And leave it be.

386
00:35:59,255 --> 00:36:00,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

387
00:36:00,155 --> 00:36:01,275
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll have to think on that.

388
00:36:01,715 --> 00:36:02,216
[SPEAKER_01]: But thank you.

389
00:36:03,136 --> 00:36:04,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Just a quick note.

390
00:36:04,636 --> 00:36:19,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you say that this self-determination or self-definition, it is sort of a constant and very fast exercise in accumulating and combining memories?

391
00:36:19,919 --> 00:36:21,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Exercise performed by whom?

392
00:36:21,779 --> 00:36:23,759
[SPEAKER_01]: By what?

393
00:36:24,019 --> 00:36:25,299
[SPEAKER_01]: It happens really fast.

394
00:36:26,180 --> 00:36:28,420
In cases of

395
00:36:29,960 --> 00:36:33,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, all of that is unified already by the sense of self.

396
00:36:34,782 --> 00:36:35,422
[SPEAKER_01]: It happens very fast.

397
00:36:35,462 --> 00:36:38,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I believe this happens really fast in our minds.

398
00:36:38,062 --> 00:36:38,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Every

399
00:36:58,462 --> 00:37:07,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Every regaining of consciousness, this very slight moment where the memories fall back into place as mosaic piece.

400
00:37:10,212 --> 00:37:10,492
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

401
00:37:10,512 --> 00:37:14,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, how can you know then the moment before that happens?

402
00:37:15,817 --> 00:37:19,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the moment before that happens, everything is blank.

403
00:37:20,481 --> 00:37:21,381
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what are you saying?

404
00:37:21,401 --> 00:37:23,163
[SPEAKER_00]: The blankness is then the truth?

405
00:37:27,818 --> 00:37:30,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you know that that blankness is not owned?

406
00:37:30,701 --> 00:37:32,182
[SPEAKER_00]: What makes you think that it's not owned?

407
00:37:35,425 --> 00:37:36,807
[SPEAKER_01]: It's too empty to be owned.

408
00:37:37,387 --> 00:37:38,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing to call on to.

409
00:37:38,668 --> 00:37:41,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you have to have the first memory and then stack them all up.

410
00:37:42,032 --> 00:37:44,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, see, the blankness is already a memory.

411
00:37:44,894 --> 00:37:47,917
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you call a first memory, it's not a first memory.

412
00:37:47,957 --> 00:37:50,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Blankness before that is a previous memory.

413
00:37:51,080 --> 00:37:52,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is then the first memory?

414
00:37:54,810 --> 00:37:55,631
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an infinite loop.

415
00:37:55,751 --> 00:37:57,973
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's impossible to see what you're referring to.

416
00:37:59,954 --> 00:38:03,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Blankness before the memory can only be known as a memory.

417
00:38:03,397 --> 00:38:04,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's a contradiction in terms.

418
00:38:08,381 --> 00:38:12,284
[SPEAKER_01]: Suppose you wake up, you know, you've had a slight case of amnesia.

419
00:38:12,645 --> 00:38:14,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Wake up, you have no idea.

420
00:38:15,507 --> 00:38:16,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Just no idea.

421
00:38:18,509 --> 00:38:18,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

422
00:38:18,870 --> 00:38:20,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, you're like a newborn baby, yeah?

423
00:38:22,012 --> 00:38:22,593
[SPEAKER_01]: The memories

424
00:38:23,154 --> 00:38:29,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, then, okay, this is where I came from, this is what I did before that, and then this cracks up really fast and there it is.

425
00:38:30,237 --> 00:38:31,597
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your personality there again.

426
00:38:32,157 --> 00:38:33,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, everything, now I can function.

427
00:38:34,678 --> 00:38:39,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, but say, say like this, say you wake up from amnesia and your memories don't come back.

428
00:38:40,741 --> 00:38:40,961
Yeah.

429
00:38:41,421 --> 00:38:42,101
[SPEAKER_00]: So what do you think?

430
00:38:42,201 --> 00:38:45,282
[SPEAKER_00]: That state after amnesia, is that state pure?

431
00:38:46,323 --> 00:38:51,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is that state equally corrupted and based on ignorance and ownership and not seeing impermanence?

432
00:38:54,292 --> 00:39:01,420
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's just by no means anywhere near enlightenment and seeing the infinite or the deathless at all.

433
00:39:01,540 --> 00:39:03,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that brings us exactly to what we said.

434
00:39:03,562 --> 00:39:07,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Memories and your sense of self that came out of it, it's relative and remains relative.

435
00:39:08,227 --> 00:39:16,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you remove it, whether you erase the memories, whether you catch the first moment before the memories, that's... all of that is already owned.

436
00:39:16,695 --> 00:39:25,921
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what the Buddha said, that even like the newborn baby, that doesn't have the memories, has not developed sensual appetites, has not corrupted itself.

437
00:39:26,442 --> 00:39:39,350
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind, the senses, are already underlined by all the asavas, by the tendencies, underlying tendencies towards sensuality, towards craving, towards views.

438
00:39:39,931 --> 00:39:42,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, it's not a question of catching the first moment,

439
00:39:43,633 --> 00:39:47,174
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the second moment, the question is abandoning the whole picture.

440
00:39:48,154 --> 00:39:48,714
The whole thing.

441
00:39:48,974 --> 00:39:54,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's... it's... you can just see that it's just... it's a... it's a conglomerate.

442
00:39:54,755 --> 00:39:55,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but it's irrelevant.

443
00:39:56,076 --> 00:40:03,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's a conglomerate, or if it's a one thing, if it happens very slowly, if it happens very fast, happens within the framework.

444
00:40:03,917 --> 00:40:06,098
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the framework that needs to be abandoned.

445
00:40:06,418 --> 00:40:08,638
[SPEAKER_00]: And framework is not happening very fast.

446
00:40:09,138 --> 00:40:11,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Framework is there from the moment you're born.

447
00:40:13,047 --> 00:40:14,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's always there, yeah.

448
00:40:14,908 --> 00:40:16,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so that's the point.

449
00:40:16,509 --> 00:40:22,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, how many memories you accumulate, and if you're trying to forget or undo, all of that is second to the problem.

450
00:40:23,534 --> 00:40:29,958
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is underlying tendencies towards ownership, that are pretty much manifested, they start from the beginning.

451
00:40:30,058 --> 00:40:40,845
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, baby has no sense of self, that has developed on account of choices and so on, yet it keeps acting out of these underlying tendencies, which is why it develops the sense of self.

452
00:40:41,695 --> 00:40:54,445
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep saying, the sense of self is not the problem, it's the symptom of an underlying problem, acting out of pleasurable feeling, acting out of neutral feeling, acting out of uncomfortable feeling.

453
00:40:55,385 --> 00:40:59,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of self will be generated one way or the other.

454
00:40:59,428 --> 00:41:10,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you zoom in, if you really explore what's happening there at that level, look at the taṇha behind this, this magnetism, really zoom into that,

455
00:41:11,654 --> 00:41:12,374
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no, no.

456
00:41:12,434 --> 00:41:13,355
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to zoom out.

457
00:41:15,296 --> 00:41:15,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Framework.

458
00:41:16,396 --> 00:41:17,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Zoom out.

459
00:41:17,237 --> 00:41:17,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Context.

460
00:41:18,337 --> 00:41:19,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Not zoom in.

461
00:41:21,018 --> 00:41:22,039
[SPEAKER_00]: The opposite direction.

462
00:41:22,279 --> 00:41:22,899
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I'm saying.

463
00:41:23,019 --> 00:41:25,521
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about catching the moment before or after.

464
00:41:26,041 --> 00:41:30,043
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about zooming out far enough to see the framework there.

465
00:41:35,485 --> 00:41:36,706
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where dhamma is.

466
00:41:38,707 --> 00:41:40,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you see that dhamma in

467
00:41:42,063 --> 00:41:43,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Zooming out as well.

468
00:41:43,323 --> 00:41:47,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe you will, because it's probably... That's the only place you will see it.

469
00:41:47,705 --> 00:41:49,886
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... dhamma is not a momentary response.

470
00:41:50,826 --> 00:41:56,768
[SPEAKER_00]: Dhamma is basically the whole direction of your mind already on the level of that framework.

471
00:42:00,029 --> 00:42:03,530
Okay, maybe... maybe we're talking at cross purposes here.

472
00:42:03,550 --> 00:42:03,791
I...

473
00:42:04,051 --> 00:42:07,872
[SPEAKER_01]: I was thinking of exploring a certain emotion

474
00:42:09,733 --> 00:42:16,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Really feeling it, really going in there, into that, eh, the feeling, really exploring, exploring it from within.

475
00:42:17,779 --> 00:42:23,923
[SPEAKER_01]: And at a certain point, you will feel this, this humongous pull, the pull of personality.

476
00:42:24,964 --> 00:42:31,609
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you feel like you're in contact... No, I think we're talking about the same thing, but I keep trying to point the opposite direction of what you're saying.

477
00:42:32,770 --> 00:42:36,032
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you don't explore the feeling by getting into the feeling.

478
00:42:37,407 --> 00:42:43,053
[SPEAKER_00]: That basically means making it even more particular, which means falling away from the nature of the feeling.

479
00:42:44,755 --> 00:42:47,678
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to zoom out.

480
00:42:47,818 --> 00:42:52,122
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of going to feel it more, you zoom out to know it more.

481
00:42:52,423 --> 00:42:55,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Know what's already there, whichever way it's all there.

482
00:42:55,646 --> 00:42:58,949
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to make it felt differently to get to understand it better.

483
00:42:59,907 --> 00:43:03,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever way it's felt, you want to know it to that extent.

484
00:43:03,510 --> 00:43:05,051
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the framework I'm talking about.

485
00:43:05,432 --> 00:43:08,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the opposite of falling onto the particular level.

486
00:43:09,175 --> 00:43:14,179
[SPEAKER_00]: It's staying on that general, the background I often talk about.

487
00:43:16,441 --> 00:43:18,722
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the taṇha is.

488
00:43:18,742 --> 00:43:19,823
That's where the taṇha is, yeah.

489
00:43:21,765 --> 00:43:22,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, next question.

490
00:43:26,409 --> 00:43:29,271
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, eh... Bhante, thank you again for, eh...

491
00:43:29,722 --> 00:43:32,424
[SPEAKER_02]: Your willingness to give this question an answer.

492
00:43:33,145 --> 00:43:35,987
[SPEAKER_02]: This is something that is quite interesting to me.

493
00:43:36,047 --> 00:43:48,717
[SPEAKER_02]: Namely, to what degree, if at all, it's even possible to meaningfully talk about self within the Buddhist framework.

494
00:43:50,619 --> 00:43:56,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, from what I've heard from your, some of your talks, it's not...

495
00:43:56,644 --> 00:43:57,725
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people have this very

496
00:43:58,649 --> 00:44:22,497
[SPEAKER_02]: I would say, simplistic understanding of what non-self is supposed to be, often it is understood either as a simple negation of the self, or they go down the Humean line, where they think that basically it's more or less a mosaic of, you know, aggregates, and then they say, well, there's no self, because in every moment of experience, you have these constituents.

497
00:44:22,517 --> 00:44:24,677
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you break this down, this is who I am.

498
00:44:25,057 --> 00:44:26,138
[SPEAKER_02]: But this, this is just,

499
00:44:27,654 --> 00:44:34,299
[SPEAKER_02]: I would say a very experientially naïve way of thinking about it.

500
00:44:34,859 --> 00:44:47,548
[SPEAKER_02]: But the thing is, for instance, when I was reading this sutta, All the Taints, it ends up with a list of basically all the views that are wrong with regards to the self.

501
00:44:48,088 --> 00:44:55,173
[SPEAKER_02]: So, all the views that arise from essential desire, being, and ignorance.

502
00:44:57,756 --> 00:44:58,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Can I read them out?

503
00:44:58,897 --> 00:44:59,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

504
00:45:00,378 --> 00:45:00,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

505
00:45:00,659 --> 00:45:02,880
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he says, eh...

506
00:45:03,521 --> 00:45:09,586
[SPEAKER_02]: These are the six views that arise in someone who basically is ignorant, if we oversimplify.

507
00:45:10,327 --> 00:45:11,668
[SPEAKER_02]: Self exists for me.

508
00:45:12,469 --> 00:45:13,990
[SPEAKER_02]: No self exists for me.

509
00:45:14,591 --> 00:45:16,172
[SPEAKER_02]: I perceive self with self.

510
00:45:16,752 --> 00:45:18,674
[SPEAKER_02]: I perceive not-self with self.

511
00:45:19,094 --> 00:45:20,876
[SPEAKER_02]: I perceive self with not-self.

512
00:45:21,236 --> 00:45:22,938
[SPEAKER_02]: And everlasting, enduring self.

513
00:45:23,198 --> 00:45:23,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

514
00:45:23,678 --> 00:45:25,160
[SPEAKER_02]: So, everlasting, enduring self.

515
00:45:25,220 --> 00:45:25,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

516
00:45:26,230 --> 00:45:27,171
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's put this aside.

517
00:45:27,211 --> 00:45:38,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's say that, you know, for the most part, even in most of, say, Western thought nowadays, there will be very few people who would be willing to adopt some view of this type.

518
00:45:38,799 --> 00:45:45,804
[SPEAKER_02]: But the other views are either present in everyday experience or in most of philosophical accounts that you would find.

519
00:45:45,844 --> 00:45:49,346
[SPEAKER_02]: For instance, you might say someone like

520
00:45:51,287 --> 00:45:59,130
[SPEAKER_02]: Kusar would say that, you know, I perceive self with self, so you have a transcendental self that perceives the empirical self.

521
00:45:59,350 --> 00:46:05,572
[SPEAKER_02]: Or many similar might say, okay, well, this transcendental self is not really a self, it's a non-self.

522
00:46:05,753 --> 00:46:06,573
[SPEAKER_02]: But all this is wrong.

523
00:46:06,633 --> 00:46:16,157
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I'm just curious, you know, there would seem that there is a certain place for the self, so to speak.

524
00:46:16,397 --> 00:46:19,878
[SPEAKER_02]: It is naive to say that non-self is simply a negation of

525
00:46:21,695 --> 00:46:24,557
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm not particularly keen on the idea that it's not.

526
00:46:24,617 --> 00:46:25,317
[SPEAKER_02]: In the past, I would be.

527
00:46:25,337 --> 00:46:26,038
[SPEAKER_02]: But now,

528
00:46:50,998 --> 00:46:58,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah.

529
00:46:59,184 --> 00:46:59,864
[SPEAKER_00]: There is self.

530
00:47:00,905 --> 00:47:01,646
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not yours.

531
00:47:02,266 --> 00:47:02,666
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

532
00:47:03,006 --> 00:47:05,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha never said there is no self.

533
00:47:06,729 --> 00:47:08,750
[SPEAKER_00]: He said that which is self is not yours.

534
00:47:09,411 --> 00:47:10,392
[SPEAKER_02]: And there's a big difference.

535
00:47:12,193 --> 00:47:13,494
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's removing the ownership.

536
00:47:14,692 --> 00:47:15,973
[SPEAKER_00]: It will remove the sense of self.

537
00:47:16,253 --> 00:47:19,614
[SPEAKER_00]: People... people think I own things because I am.

538
00:47:20,074 --> 00:47:20,254
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

539
00:47:20,534 --> 00:47:21,455
[SPEAKER_00]: It's other way around.

540
00:47:22,275 --> 00:47:28,057
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you keep taking things as mine through sensuality, aversion and ignorance.

541
00:47:28,097 --> 00:47:29,077
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you are.

542
00:47:30,518 --> 00:47:34,139
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you have generated that sense of self that you cannot step outside of.

543
00:47:34,199 --> 00:47:34,799
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a symptom.

544
00:47:36,660 --> 00:47:37,901
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a symptom of ownership.

545
00:47:37,981 --> 00:47:41,662
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove the ownership, sense of self cannot persist, or not to the same extent.

546
00:47:43,384 --> 00:47:44,805
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why ātavāda is a view.

547
00:47:45,946 --> 00:47:53,073
[SPEAKER_00]: See, there is a sense of self as a result of careless ownership, not seeing impermanence, not restraining your actions.

548
00:47:54,094 --> 00:47:58,939
[SPEAKER_00]: And that self that has been generated through such actions, you put first.

549
00:47:59,419 --> 00:48:00,400
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the ātavāda.

550
00:48:00,700 --> 00:48:02,322
[SPEAKER_00]: You think that is the first thing.

551
00:48:02,922 --> 00:48:05,565
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I own things because I am.

552
00:48:08,723 --> 00:48:12,386
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's because you keep owning individual things, that's why you are.

553
00:48:12,626 --> 00:48:16,249
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that sense of self is there, and that's why the view on top of it is there.

554
00:48:20,112 --> 00:48:28,459
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a... you don't develop the experience of not-myself through figuring it out.

555
00:48:29,220 --> 00:48:33,964
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop it through purifying your behavior that has been feeding it and sustaining it.

556
00:48:35,622 --> 00:48:38,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you'll see that that sense of self does not belong to you.

557
00:48:38,783 --> 00:48:40,944
[SPEAKER_00]: And thus, it cannot be called self, truly.

558
00:48:42,664 --> 00:48:46,946
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why when people came to the Buddha and said, ah, so Master Gautama says there is no self.

559
00:48:48,006 --> 00:48:48,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And he would be quiet.

560
00:48:48,846 --> 00:48:55,188
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't even reply to that because it's a completely... well, it's a question completely based on that wrong assumption that they don't see.

561
00:48:56,029 --> 00:48:58,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, so there is a sense of self that Master Gautama knows.

562
00:48:58,629 --> 00:49:00,310
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't... he would reply to that either.

563
00:49:02,410 --> 00:49:12,113
[SPEAKER_00]: But practically speaking, that sense of self that is yours, remove that part of this is mine, and it won't be sense of self anymore.

564
00:49:12,573 --> 00:49:16,154
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't try to remove the whole thing by saying, oh, the self doesn't exist.

565
00:49:16,774 --> 00:49:20,095
[SPEAKER_00]: If it doesn't exist, what are you referring to as non-existing?

566
00:49:21,935 --> 00:49:26,337
[SPEAKER_00]: There has to be something that's being designated when people say, oh, there is no self.

567
00:49:26,357 --> 00:49:28,937
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that whole, the view permutation.

568
00:49:29,398 --> 00:49:30,618
[SPEAKER_00]: With self, he sees self.

569
00:49:31,860 --> 00:49:39,282
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be... sense of self has to be either on immediacy level or on the level of reflection.

570
00:49:39,322 --> 00:49:42,823
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he were to undo the ownership,

571
00:49:52,970 --> 00:49:55,312
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the ownership part is what makes it the self.

572
00:49:55,332 --> 00:49:57,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that thing that is there, taken to be the self.

573
00:50:20,217 --> 00:50:28,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when the Buddha said, what is it that, you know, Putujana takes a self, oh, it's the form, it's the feeling, it's the perception, it's the intentions and the consciousness.

574
00:50:29,045 --> 00:50:29,625
[SPEAKER_00]: There is nothing.

575
00:50:29,725 --> 00:50:38,553
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all the phenomena generally, your sense of self will be resting upon something, because your sense of self will be resting upon the ownership of something.

576
00:50:44,538 --> 00:50:46,940
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if I understand correctly, if

577
00:50:47,989 --> 00:50:58,739
[SPEAKER_03]: You remove this ownership, you also remove the attachments you have to it, and therefore you would see it more objectively.

578
00:50:58,779 --> 00:51:00,861
[SPEAKER_03]: Is this the difference?

579
00:51:00,901 --> 00:51:01,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Sort of.

580
00:51:01,362 --> 00:51:11,411
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically if you say, you know, you keep eating food that doesn't agree with you, and your whole body is suffering, you're having headaches, and who knows what else, and you don't know the reason.

581
00:51:11,651 --> 00:51:13,053
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know that it's because of the food.

582
00:51:14,237 --> 00:51:19,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, you... sometimes you have a headache like this, but if you concentrate on something else, headache disappears, then you think maybe...

583
00:51:20,400 --> 00:51:23,741
[SPEAKER_00]: But either way, you keep eating the food that it's making your condition worse.

584
00:51:23,841 --> 00:51:25,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you find out that that was the problem.

585
00:51:26,082 --> 00:51:28,183
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop eating the food that was bothering you.

586
00:51:29,944 --> 00:51:36,287
[SPEAKER_00]: Sufficient time needs to pass, basically, until the body recovers, and the headache will slowly dissipate and not come back.

587
00:51:37,598 --> 00:51:39,359
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what will happen to the sense of self.

588
00:51:40,240 --> 00:51:42,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that you're gonna lose your head that used to hurt.

589
00:51:43,322 --> 00:51:47,104
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just gonna lose the pain of that which still remains there.

590
00:51:47,864 --> 00:51:53,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that sense of self, that sense of an individual that's still making choices, that remains.

591
00:51:54,636 --> 00:51:57,837
[SPEAKER_00]: But the emotional, eh... ownership of it, that's gone.

592
00:51:58,017 --> 00:51:58,977
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is an arahant.

593
00:51:59,157 --> 00:52:07,320
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what people cannot under... think... or either cannot understand, or think arahant is this spaced-out robot, unaware of himself.

594
00:52:08,180 --> 00:52:12,401
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he still makes choices, he still prefers, he still has agreeable and disagreeable.

595
00:52:12,861 --> 00:52:15,042
[SPEAKER_00]: He has no ownership of any of it, top to bottom.

596
00:52:15,862 --> 00:52:19,003
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why he cannot suffer, because he's not involved with the wrong view.

597
00:52:24,012 --> 00:52:25,833
Would you say that this...

598
00:52:26,693 --> 00:52:32,694
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is something I read in the Sutta, so I thought it was really nice.

599
00:52:33,334 --> 00:52:36,195
[SPEAKER_01]: That the sense of self is actually a corruption of the mind.

600
00:52:37,455 --> 00:52:39,175
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a corruption.

601
00:52:39,656 --> 00:52:44,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind, without the self, is just a sense of self.

602
00:52:46,077 --> 00:52:49,858
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha says it's malleable, wielding and radiant.

603
00:52:50,878 --> 00:52:51,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

604
00:52:51,454 --> 00:53:01,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, but see, if somebody is not free from the sense of self, their thought of a mind that's malleable, radiant, is taken as their self.

605
00:53:01,960 --> 00:53:03,741
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, that's not what that mind is.

606
00:53:05,322 --> 00:53:12,266
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, now, you are perceiving the idea of not-self, mind free from self, with your current self.

607
00:53:13,387 --> 00:53:15,568
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's one of the views that's read out.

608
00:53:16,789 --> 00:53:18,550
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you cannot even, theoretically,

609
00:53:19,709 --> 00:53:28,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Understand mind free from the sense of self, free from the ownership, unless you have developed non-ownership here and now.

610
00:53:29,112 --> 00:53:31,552
[SPEAKER_01]: I received another question, very much related to this.

611
00:53:32,013 --> 00:53:36,474
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the question goes, if nothing is yours, what is then your mind?

612
00:53:37,494 --> 00:53:43,756
[SPEAKER_01]: If nothing is left, how do you train your mind or what do you train your mind with?

613
00:53:43,776 --> 00:53:45,916
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean nothing is left?

614
00:53:46,817 --> 00:53:47,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is left.

615
00:53:48,714 --> 00:53:49,815
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't touched anything.

616
00:53:50,455 --> 00:53:53,296
[SPEAKER_00]: You just removed your gratuitous emotional ownership of it.

617
00:53:54,017 --> 00:53:56,018
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to destroy things to not own them.

618
00:53:57,218 --> 00:54:01,520
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to stop regarding them as yours, mentally.

619
00:54:01,540 --> 00:54:01,761
[SPEAKER_03]: No.

620
00:54:04,562 --> 00:54:04,902
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay.

621
00:54:10,645 --> 00:54:13,346
[SPEAKER_03]: Because if they're not yours, they're not your problem.

622
00:54:14,147 --> 00:54:14,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

623
00:54:15,667 --> 00:54:22,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, imagine you have that attitude towards your own senses, towards your own feeling, towards your own intentions, towards your own mind.

624
00:54:22,450 --> 00:54:23,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Imperturbable attitude.

625
00:54:23,851 --> 00:54:28,693
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you cannot forget that it cannot be yours, even if you want it to be yours.

626
00:54:28,913 --> 00:54:29,374
[SPEAKER_00]: It's impossible.

627
00:54:29,394 --> 00:54:30,254
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

628
00:54:30,334 --> 00:54:31,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Ownership becomes inconceivable.

629
00:54:32,915 --> 00:54:37,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you thoroughly understood the danger, the peril, the escape, and so on.

630
00:54:37,477 --> 00:54:40,379
[SPEAKER_00]: The impermanence, the un-ownability of these things.

631
00:54:40,939 --> 00:54:44,881
[SPEAKER_00]: You fully understood and you can... you don't even need to keep telling to yourself, oh, this is not mine.

632
00:54:47,740 --> 00:54:49,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nothing can disturb you, then, at all.

633
00:54:49,822 --> 00:54:50,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely inconceivable.

634
00:54:51,104 --> 00:54:58,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering or any perturbance cannot be even perceived anymore.

635
00:54:58,412 --> 00:54:59,713
I don't try saying...

636
00:54:59,933 --> 00:55:01,655
I'm sorry...

637
00:55:02,676 --> 00:55:05,379
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't try... said somewhere...

638
00:55:07,476 --> 00:55:19,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time... so you have these problems, the phone keeps ringing, and every time the phone rings, you pick it up and say, hi, this is Mr. Smith, and... and he said, well, just stop picking up the phone and stop thinking you're Mr. Smith.

639
00:55:21,400 --> 00:55:24,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, you can't help, you can't stop thinking who you are.

640
00:55:24,400 --> 00:55:26,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Can't stop picking up the phone.

641
00:55:26,361 --> 00:55:34,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's not a matter of choice, but you can certainly stop owning how Mr. Smith feels and not acting out of the feeling when you pick up the phone.

642
00:55:36,727 --> 00:55:44,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, even that Mr. Smith will fade away.

643
00:55:45,074 --> 00:56:05,530
[SPEAKER_02]: Could you, in a certain sense, say, because this act of ownership is interesting, could you say, then, that maybe one way of putting this would be to say, to realize that the structure of the self, so, or the sense of self, is something that is embedded on a

644
00:56:06,400 --> 00:56:10,943
[SPEAKER_02]: In a larger picture, in a larger process, which is basically selfless.

645
00:56:11,424 --> 00:56:15,306
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there's already this sense of...

646
00:56:15,326 --> 00:56:18,729
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the act of ownership, the process of ownership, right?

647
00:56:18,989 --> 00:56:22,371
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there's a slight potential danger here.

648
00:56:22,431 --> 00:56:27,575
[SPEAKER_02]: You might say that, well, you know, where is this sense or process of ownership nested?

649
00:56:28,195 --> 00:56:32,378
[SPEAKER_02]: So, who is it that wants to own?

650
00:56:33,079 --> 00:56:33,679
That's a problem.

651
00:56:34,011 --> 00:56:39,814
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in a certain sense, you kind of have to realize that this is a bottomless process, so to speak.

652
00:56:39,854 --> 00:56:46,498
[SPEAKER_02]: It is a process that does not have yet another self underneath it that would like to grasp.

653
00:56:47,139 --> 00:56:47,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

654
00:56:48,019 --> 00:56:49,600
[SPEAKER_00]: In that sense, it's beginningless.

655
00:56:49,780 --> 00:56:51,261
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a vicious circle.

656
00:56:52,802 --> 00:56:58,585
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, like, you have a headache because of the food you eat, and you keep eating the food because

657
00:57:04,815 --> 00:57:10,977
[SPEAKER_00]: So, headache is not a problem, it's a symptom, but at the same time, becomes a problem, because you keep acting out of it.

658
00:57:14,018 --> 00:57:19,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop acting out of it, it's going to be seen as a symptom, and eventually you're going to discover what the real problem is.

659
00:57:19,600 --> 00:57:25,722
[SPEAKER_00]: You kept thinking that's the problem, and you kept acting out of it, and you kept doing things, they were feeding it in return.

660
00:57:26,583 --> 00:57:27,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Ironically enough.

661
00:57:33,715 --> 00:57:42,523
[SPEAKER_02]: In the Buddhist perspective, so the overall general framework, the question as to how and why this came to be is a known question, so to speak.

662
00:57:42,743 --> 00:57:43,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

663
00:57:43,104 --> 00:57:47,388
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a question of, like when Buddha said, imagine a man being shot by an arrow.

664
00:57:48,128 --> 00:57:48,328
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

665
00:57:48,509 --> 00:57:55,255
[SPEAKER_00]: But instead of allowing that arrow to be pulled out and wound healed, he won't allow it until he finds out who shot him.

666
00:57:56,003 --> 00:57:56,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Why they did so?

667
00:57:57,484 --> 00:58:00,066
[SPEAKER_00]: What did he do to deserve that?

668
00:58:00,206 --> 00:58:01,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there anything that he could have avoided?

669
00:58:01,627 --> 00:58:05,670
[SPEAKER_00]: What clan was the man that he... all of these irrelevant things while he's dying.

670
00:58:07,491 --> 00:58:09,692
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to realize where the priority is.

671
00:58:10,153 --> 00:58:11,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Getting the arrow out.

672
00:58:11,574 --> 00:58:14,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter who, why or what shot me.

673
00:58:15,136 --> 00:58:17,478
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is I'm shot and I'm bleeding out.

674
00:58:39,079 --> 00:58:41,000
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, I would have a question.

675
00:58:42,421 --> 00:58:43,541
[SPEAKER_02]: Or did you want to say something?

676
00:58:44,402 --> 00:58:51,025
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I was just trying to fill the silence with some random ramblings, so thank you.

677
00:58:51,986 --> 00:58:58,810
[SPEAKER_02]: We just said that we're not supposed to do that.

678
00:58:59,910 --> 00:59:01,891
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so I'm just...

679
00:59:03,332 --> 00:59:08,515
[SPEAKER_02]: I've been really interested in this notion of

680
00:59:11,074 --> 00:59:13,336
[SPEAKER_02]: ayoniso, manasikāra.

681
00:59:16,119 --> 00:59:27,290
[SPEAKER_02]: First of all, I find it fascinating how basically, for example, in the West, you know, just one particular notion, sati, became the main focus of interest and it's all about mindfulness and everything with mindfulness.

682
00:59:28,117 --> 00:59:31,420
[SPEAKER_03]: Can I just, sorry, can you also explain the word?

683
00:59:31,460 --> 00:59:37,065
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I don't know some of these foreign words and then I just lose the track of the... Yeah, yeah, don't worry.

684
00:59:37,405 --> 00:59:39,246
[SPEAKER_02]: That was the general idea.

685
00:59:39,447 --> 00:59:42,869
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I didn't want to just jump in.

686
00:59:43,370 --> 00:59:45,151
[SPEAKER_02]: This is just a preface or background.

687
00:59:45,271 --> 00:59:54,579
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what I meant is to say, even if at this point nobody knew what this term meant, is that there are a lot of very interesting

688
00:59:54,969 --> 01:00:06,152
[SPEAKER_02]: There are so many fascinating terms and notions in the Pali Canon, and I found it just fascinating how one of them was taken out, and it's now just all the rage.

689
01:00:06,493 --> 01:00:10,974
[SPEAKER_02]: But, with regards to yoniso manasikāra...

690
01:00:12,014 --> 01:00:18,776
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if I understood correctly, yoniso refers etymologically to the word for womb.

691
01:00:18,816 --> 01:00:19,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

692
01:00:21,942 --> 01:00:25,983
[SPEAKER_02]: And, it is usually translated as wisely or properly.

693
01:00:26,583 --> 01:00:26,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

694
01:00:27,103 --> 01:00:34,745
[SPEAKER_02]: And, manasikāra is basically, a literal translation would be mental act, or mentation, so to speak.

695
01:00:35,185 --> 01:00:35,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

696
01:00:35,325 --> 01:00:35,966
[SPEAKER_02]: Mentations.

697
01:00:36,286 --> 01:00:36,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

698
01:00:37,426 --> 01:00:41,147
[SPEAKER_02]: But, it is often translated, for some reason, as attention.

699
01:00:41,887 --> 01:00:43,928
[SPEAKER_02]: So, eh...

700
01:00:44,028 --> 01:00:47,809
[SPEAKER_02]: I listened to a talk you gave on this, and I wanted to re-listen it this week.

701
01:00:47,849 --> 01:00:49,409
[SPEAKER_02]: Unfortunately, I was unable to.

702
01:00:49,883 --> 01:00:54,166
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I would just want to, first of all, hear your... how would you translate it?

703
01:00:54,206 --> 01:01:02,913
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, if I remember correctly, you translated it as wise reflection, which I think is closer to what I was reading specifically.

704
01:01:02,973 --> 01:01:03,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Did I?

705
01:01:03,414 --> 01:01:06,576
[SPEAKER_00]: I might have mentioned it, but I don't think I would translate it as wise reflection.

706
01:01:06,716 --> 01:01:07,257
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

707
01:01:07,297 --> 01:01:07,797
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, no.

708
01:01:07,917 --> 01:01:11,340
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be attention, or rather, attending from the origin.

709
01:01:11,920 --> 01:01:12,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's say like that.

710
01:01:12,561 --> 01:01:13,642
[SPEAKER_02]: Attending from the origin.

711
01:01:13,702 --> 01:01:17,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, it's a subtle thing, whichever way you translate it, you're going to have to be explaining it.

712
01:01:18,439 --> 01:01:26,082
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not going to become self-evident on account of your choice of the terms you use for it, because it's too subtle to be understood.

713
01:01:26,122 --> 01:01:27,843
[SPEAKER_00]: It's one of the prerequisites for the right view.

714
01:01:28,743 --> 01:01:32,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's a hard thing to clarify for every mind individually.

715
01:01:33,725 --> 01:01:34,546
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's exactly that.

716
01:01:34,586 --> 01:01:39,888
[SPEAKER_00]: If you stop and think about it, you always, you know, people always approach things by attending to them.

717
01:01:40,928 --> 01:01:44,570
[SPEAKER_00]: That's, okay, so I look at this, I think of this, I perceive this.

718
01:01:46,340 --> 01:01:49,721
[SPEAKER_00]: womb of that act is what yoniso manasit.

719
01:01:49,781 --> 01:01:50,702
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the origin.

720
01:01:51,382 --> 01:02:02,926
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, if you attend to the womb now, of the origin, that becomes the thing you're attending to, which means the womb of that attention is, again, behind you.

721
01:02:03,826 --> 01:02:06,907
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's learning to see that behind, without attending it.

722
01:02:07,147 --> 01:02:07,927
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you do that?

723
01:02:08,448 --> 01:02:09,208
[SPEAKER_00]: By knowing it.

724
01:02:09,949 --> 01:02:16,253
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why context, background, peripheral awareness, all these things I'm talking about, it's all that.

725
01:02:16,353 --> 01:02:20,976
[SPEAKER_00]: It just points at the same thing and that's where the knowledge is.

726
01:02:21,076 --> 01:02:28,881
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's so important to realize that all the rage and mainstream practice, it's completely misdirected.

727
01:02:28,981 --> 01:02:34,865
[SPEAKER_00]: It's completely based on perceiving and seeking and seeing and particularizing more and more and more

728
01:02:36,635 --> 01:02:37,416
[SPEAKER_00]: All that's irrelevant.

729
01:02:38,236 --> 01:02:43,761
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is finding more details at the expense of the context that you don't even know to begin with.

730
01:02:44,662 --> 01:02:46,444
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you do, you wouldn't need any of the...

731
01:02:46,844 --> 01:02:51,748
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to know what shape the arrow was, and how it flew, and the physics behind it.

732
01:02:52,629 --> 01:02:56,272
[SPEAKER_00]: All I need to know is that it hurts here and now, and I'm resisting the pain.

733
01:02:56,913 --> 01:02:57,273
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

734
01:02:57,834 --> 01:03:00,636
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the detail level that you need to have.

735
01:03:01,417 --> 01:03:02,518
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't need to know anything else.

736
01:03:04,911 --> 01:03:10,653
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the tendency to not just attend, but to over-attend and attend even more what you attended to.

737
01:03:11,113 --> 01:03:14,934
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, when you tell them, no, it's the origin of the attention, ah, so they attend to that.

738
01:03:16,194 --> 01:03:19,195
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yeah, but now there is something else as the origin of that attention.

739
01:03:19,955 --> 01:03:21,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you know it without attending it?

740
01:03:23,636 --> 01:03:26,557
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole practice of learning how to see it peripherally.

741
01:03:27,497 --> 01:03:29,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what the experience is.

742
01:03:30,338 --> 01:03:31,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Peripheral and actual.

743
01:03:33,467 --> 01:03:36,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you attend to it or not, that is the basis for your attention.

744
01:03:37,910 --> 01:03:52,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, whatever you're attending now, on account of your senses, is possible, only because your senses, in the form of this body, are still there, simultaneously with what you're attending to, yet you're completely unaware of them.

745
01:03:53,700 --> 01:03:59,303
[SPEAKER_00]: So, learning to be aware, without needing to attend to the senses, because that's impossible.

746
01:04:00,944 --> 01:04:02,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Learning to know that there is an I,

747
01:04:06,405 --> 01:04:16,192
[SPEAKER_00]: But if your measure of mindfulness is what you attend closely, you'll never develop this mindfulness.

748
01:04:16,292 --> 01:04:18,474
[SPEAKER_00]: How can your eye attend itself?

749
01:04:19,591 --> 01:04:20,491
[SPEAKER_00]: But looking in the mirror?

750
01:04:20,571 --> 01:04:20,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

751
01:04:21,032 --> 01:04:28,194
[SPEAKER_00]: You're attending to the reflection of the eye, for which presence of the eye is necessary, you remain equally unaware of the context.

752
01:04:28,894 --> 01:04:30,255
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's yoniso manasikāra.

753
01:04:30,795 --> 01:04:39,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Discerning that which cannot be attended, yet it is there as a necessary basis, is what that wise attention is.

754
01:04:41,395 --> 01:04:54,085
[SPEAKER_00]: And the problem with wise or proper attention, it's not that it's etymologically wrong to translate like that, but the problem is people will think, all right, so I am engaging with the practice of yoniso manasikāra when I stop and think about things.

755
01:04:55,265 --> 01:04:55,446
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

756
01:04:57,927 --> 01:04:59,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Or certainly not necessarily.

757
01:05:01,270 --> 01:05:07,915
[SPEAKER_00]: So if I just think about things a bit more, that's what the Buddha meant by yoniso manasikāra, which is a prerequisite for sotāpatti.

758
01:05:10,116 --> 01:05:12,758
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's a bit more than that.

759
01:05:16,801 --> 01:05:27,829
[SPEAKER_02]: It is precisely for this reason, because of what you've just described, that I found this idea of reflection or the term reflection so useful, you know.

760
01:05:27,869 --> 01:05:33,874
[SPEAKER_02]: Precisely this notion where you have... you're not engrossed in the object that is given to you.

761
01:05:34,153 --> 01:05:40,156
[SPEAKER_02]: But you're basically trying to capture the whole setting of it, so the dynamics of your intending towards the object.

762
01:05:40,196 --> 01:05:49,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're trying to capture the whole setting, like the alertness, the German word that Heidegger used, without making it an object.

763
01:05:50,161 --> 01:05:50,921
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the key.

764
01:05:51,662 --> 01:05:54,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even Heidegger made it an object.

765
01:05:55,517 --> 01:05:57,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the tendency of the mind.

766
01:05:57,999 --> 01:06:05,023
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the context, some people more, some people less, but then you take that for granted and that becomes your object.

767
01:06:05,423 --> 01:06:08,265
[SPEAKER_00]: And you lose context in regard to that.

768
01:06:09,146 --> 01:06:12,368
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you want to capture the setting without attending it.

769
01:06:13,192 --> 01:06:18,813
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's all about... it's not about finding the first context, or the first original object, or something.

770
01:06:19,313 --> 01:06:30,435
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about fully purifying and clarifying and removing any slightest traces of ignorance of the relationship between what you're attending, at any given time, and the necessary basis for that attention.

771
01:06:30,715 --> 01:06:31,075
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

772
01:06:31,695 --> 01:06:36,216
[SPEAKER_00]: You understand, with this, this is, all the avijjā is undone.

773
01:06:36,436 --> 01:06:38,496
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhakti-śamupāda fulfilled, completed.

774
01:06:40,877 --> 01:06:42,737
[SPEAKER_00]: With the presence of this, this is.

775
01:06:44,826 --> 01:06:48,809
[SPEAKER_00]: With the presence of the body as the base for the senses, there is sight.

776
01:06:49,389 --> 01:06:51,971
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what I'm looking at, because the body is there.

777
01:06:53,812 --> 01:07:00,856
[SPEAKER_00]: So, knowing that relationship, fully understanding it, that's what needs to be done.

778
01:07:01,757 --> 01:07:14,585
[SPEAKER_02]: If we go now back to what we were talking before about the breathing meditation, could we say that in a certain sense, for example, when you have mindfulness of breath and the body in general, what you're basically doing is precisely

779
01:07:15,103 --> 01:07:23,611
[SPEAKER_02]: You're going to the very, let's say, in a certain sense, foundation, the very basis on which you can basically build this.

780
01:07:23,651 --> 01:07:32,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's, in a certain sense, you're kind of going as as deep as you can go with the dynamic, with this dynamics that you were mentioning.

781
01:07:32,879 --> 01:07:38,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Because breath, as a living, as long as you're alive, breath will be there, the body posture will be there.

782
01:07:38,303 --> 01:07:42,287
[SPEAKER_02]: So there is always like, you know, embodied

783
01:07:43,627 --> 01:07:45,388
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

784
01:07:45,528 --> 01:07:50,729
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

785
01:07:50,849 --> 01:08:00,572
[SPEAKER_02]: For as long as it stays the background.

786
01:08:00,893 --> 01:08:01,513
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

787
01:08:02,133 --> 01:08:11,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's too easy, see, to start attending to the breath and the body as an object of your attention, which means it's not a reference on a background level anymore.

788
01:08:12,613 --> 01:08:26,836
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, if you know it as being there while experiencing this, or knowing it as being there while experiencing that, you are practicing seeing it as a reference and studying your mind in that knowledge.

789
01:08:28,070 --> 01:08:29,651
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, yes, you don't need more than that.

790
01:08:30,171 --> 01:08:30,872
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Buddha said.

791
01:08:31,412 --> 01:08:34,755
[SPEAKER_00]: All these different, you know, satipaṭṭhāna of this, mindfulness of that.

792
01:08:35,275 --> 01:08:39,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, he's mindful of the body to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

793
01:08:39,678 --> 01:08:40,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Just that.

794
01:08:40,338 --> 01:08:43,240
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to the extent necessary, you have fully understood this context.

795
01:08:43,781 --> 01:08:49,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Extent necessary for uprooting of passion, aversion and the need for distraction and ownership.

796
01:08:49,424 --> 01:08:49,905
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

797
01:08:52,266 --> 01:08:54,288
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the question would be...

798
01:08:56,584 --> 01:09:00,045
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, so I find this very helpful.

799
01:09:00,565 --> 01:09:12,530
[SPEAKER_02]: But the question would be, would it be right to say that, as an intermediary step, so to speak, you make something that is implicit explicit, so that you actually know it.

800
01:09:12,590 --> 01:09:18,573
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what I mean by that is, everybody, in a certain sense, know that he or she breathes, right?

801
01:09:18,593 --> 01:09:23,455
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to make it in the sense that you become aware and that you

802
01:09:23,983 --> 01:09:24,604
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand?

803
01:09:24,964 --> 01:09:29,368
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you move it into the periphery, but the quality has changed.

804
01:09:29,508 --> 01:09:30,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

805
01:09:30,629 --> 01:09:36,073
[SPEAKER_00]: If that's what you were told you're supposed to do, but people are told the opposite.

806
01:09:36,293 --> 01:09:42,138
[SPEAKER_00]: You're supposed to attend it even closer, and even closer, and catch even more specific sensations.

807
01:09:43,379 --> 01:09:45,701
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were not told to do that, there would be no problem.

808
01:09:48,704 --> 01:09:52,427
[SPEAKER_00]: But, initially, whatever you do, you have to do it by attending it first.

809
01:09:54,433 --> 01:09:58,976
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the knowledge of the peripheral is there, you won't be confused in regard to that.

810
01:09:59,096 --> 01:10:00,797
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Arahant still has attention.

811
01:10:00,957 --> 01:10:04,078
[SPEAKER_00]: He still attends and chooses and, as I said, prefers and so on.

812
01:10:04,759 --> 01:10:09,762
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is no more perverting of the order and confusing and putting the second first and so on.

813
01:10:10,262 --> 01:10:10,982
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

814
01:10:16,946 --> 01:10:17,566
[SPEAKER_02]: This is great.

815
01:10:17,706 --> 01:10:20,988
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, this has been of tremendous help.

816
01:10:21,358 --> 01:10:21,718
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

817
01:10:21,758 --> 01:10:26,079
[SPEAKER_02]: So, gaining the right view is what this group and everyone should be striving for.

818
01:10:26,199 --> 01:10:26,479
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

819
01:10:26,519 --> 01:10:27,580
[SPEAKER_02]: Gaining right view.

820
01:10:27,600 --> 01:10:29,960
[SPEAKER_02]: And then we'll be able to understand everything else.

821
01:10:51,792 --> 01:10:54,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, then you will know what you need to do to be fully enlightened.

822
01:10:58,358 --> 01:11:08,347
[SPEAKER_01]: You also hear accounts of some other teachers, such as, I don't know, Advaita teachers, claiming that they have gained the right view.

823
01:11:09,288 --> 01:11:14,633
[SPEAKER_01]: And what some of them are saying seems correct, seems right.

824
01:11:15,454 --> 01:11:25,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Um, do you think... so, um, according to the suttas, once you get the right view, it's a matter of lifetimes, a few lifetimes before you actually gain enlightenment.

825
01:11:26,243 --> 01:11:33,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you gain the right view in your previous life, um, probably that's what's going to happen then.

826
01:11:33,169 --> 01:11:36,993
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to... it's going to be easier for you, probably, in this life too.

827
01:11:37,713 --> 01:11:40,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I wouldn't use the word easier.

828
01:11:42,400 --> 01:11:49,244
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just probably going to have less polluted relationship with your own suffering, so you're going to arrive at certain conclusions quicker.

829
01:11:50,945 --> 01:11:53,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're going to suffer equally, because you're still born.

830
01:11:56,949 --> 01:11:57,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

831
01:11:57,709 --> 01:11:59,911
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't mean in terms of suffering.

832
01:11:59,931 --> 01:12:11,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Just it seems that some some of these some of these individuals seem to be saying saying it correctly or yes, but but they're not even in the Buddhist tradition.

833
01:12:14,122 --> 01:12:25,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Probably, just the first step, the right view, is probably not so much... Well, yeah, I don't really know what they're saying, so I can't say, you know, they're all wrong, or yes, they're right.

834
01:12:26,677 --> 01:12:31,803
[SPEAKER_00]: But on the individual basis, on the basis of what's being said, I can tell you whether that's along the right lines or not.

835
01:12:32,943 --> 01:12:37,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Many teachers say, oh, just be here now, in the present moment, that's all you have.

836
01:12:37,565 --> 01:12:38,545
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you read the suttas.

837
01:12:38,605 --> 01:12:40,286
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, look, the suttas say the same.

838
01:12:40,366 --> 01:12:43,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, on that superficial level, it sounds the same.

839
01:12:43,748 --> 01:12:45,448
[SPEAKER_00]: But what else do those teachers say?

840
01:12:45,628 --> 01:12:46,889
[SPEAKER_00]: And what else do the suttas say?

841
01:12:47,309 --> 01:12:48,530
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you go about it?

842
01:12:49,710 --> 01:12:50,410
[SPEAKER_00]: Tell me more.

843
01:12:50,870 --> 01:12:52,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, doctor says he can cure me.

844
01:12:53,472 --> 01:12:55,352
[SPEAKER_00]: This doctor also says he can cure me.

845
01:12:55,392 --> 01:12:55,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Great.

846
01:12:56,033 --> 01:12:56,693
[SPEAKER_00]: I have options.

847
01:12:58,325 --> 01:12:59,486
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't stay there.

848
01:13:00,006 --> 01:13:03,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you go start talking to the doctor and see what you need to do.

849
01:13:04,127 --> 01:13:17,913
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the doctor starts saying some genetic nonsense and doesn't even really just gives you some... and then, well, you're already not trusting that, because surely, if you understood how to approve the illness, you would know how to do it.

850
01:13:19,653 --> 01:13:20,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's often you'll find that.

851
01:13:21,014 --> 01:13:23,775
[SPEAKER_00]: You have all these various eclectic teachers that collect

852
01:13:24,497 --> 01:13:29,781
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, all sorts of, like, Buddhism and other religions, and it does all sound same on that level.

853
01:13:29,801 --> 01:13:39,148
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, when somebody's at the bottom, they can't see the difference between, you know, halfway up the mountain and top of the mountain, because it's all just one direction up there.

854
01:13:39,408 --> 01:13:40,749
[SPEAKER_00]: It all looks high, anyway.

855
01:13:41,650 --> 01:13:48,315
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is a difference once you start walking it, like, how far you can go with it.

856
01:13:48,475 --> 01:13:53,879
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't want to ignore that, just because now it feels right, or you don't want to dismiss it either.

857
01:13:54,770 --> 01:13:57,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Just now, because it doesn't necessarily feel right.

858
01:13:57,533 --> 01:13:58,774
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to know what's behind.

859
01:13:58,834 --> 01:14:00,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, what's more to this instruction?

860
01:14:02,478 --> 01:14:07,062
[SPEAKER_00]: And same, if the instruction sounds wrong, you want to know why it is wrong.

861
01:14:07,182 --> 01:14:10,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just, oh, that's wrong because Ajahn Mahārāj said so.

862
01:14:11,406 --> 01:14:14,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, do you know for yourself that that's wrong for such and such reason?

863
01:14:15,370 --> 01:14:16,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that inadequate?

864
01:14:19,782 --> 01:14:23,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there doesn't seem to be much instruction behind these claims.

865
01:14:23,564 --> 01:14:24,784
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem then.

866
01:14:25,084 --> 01:14:29,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, claims cannot be taken... like, you should not take them for... that's the whole point.

867
01:14:29,766 --> 01:14:34,648
[SPEAKER_00]: You are making a claim towards the cure, and they're not saying how to cure yourself.

868
01:14:35,248 --> 01:14:37,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, you don't know what you're talking about.

869
01:14:37,169 --> 01:14:39,570
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you're a doctor who just pretends to be a doctor.

870
01:14:39,670 --> 01:14:44,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Doctor who just mystifies the solution, but has no actual solution for it.

871
01:14:45,902 --> 01:14:48,283
[SPEAKER_00]: And you read the suttas and it's completely the opposite.

872
01:14:48,623 --> 01:14:50,243
[SPEAKER_00]: It tells you exactly what you need to do.

873
01:14:51,364 --> 01:14:53,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And, by the way, it's said not just for other religions.

874
01:14:55,565 --> 01:15:02,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Any... you hear monks speaking, giving instructions, talking about the cure, find out how to go about it from them.

875
01:15:03,227 --> 01:15:05,248
[SPEAKER_00]: If they can't tell you, means they don't know it.

876
01:15:05,588 --> 01:15:06,348
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

877
01:15:07,029 --> 01:15:13,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if a person knows the cure, he is absolutely... it's inconceivable to not share it with you when he's talking

878
01:15:14,629 --> 01:15:16,911
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the only thing he's talking about is the cure.

879
01:15:17,751 --> 01:15:19,092
[SPEAKER_00]: But I will not tell you how to do it.

880
01:15:19,412 --> 01:15:20,773
[SPEAKER_00]: There's absolutely no reason for that.

881
01:15:21,494 --> 01:15:22,074
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unthinkable.

882
01:15:31,214 --> 01:15:31,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

883
01:15:31,594 --> 01:15:35,637
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, he's trying to convince you that you're not actually sick.

884
01:15:35,657 --> 01:15:44,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, we started this conversation on account of mutual illness, now my solution is to teach you how to delude yourself into believing you don't even have a headache.

885
01:15:44,984 --> 01:15:46,185
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a headache anyway?

886
01:15:46,765 --> 01:15:48,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

887
01:15:48,827 --> 01:15:52,069
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's always the pinnacle of most of the stuff I heard.

888
01:15:52,109 --> 01:15:54,791
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, well, there is no problem anyway.

889
01:15:54,871 --> 01:15:56,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop making a problem, there is no problem.

890
01:15:57,677 --> 01:16:02,458
[SPEAKER_00]: If that were the solution, you would have needed one sutta that says that, and end of story.

891
01:16:07,219 --> 01:16:14,481
[SPEAKER_00]: But see, many people would still think, oh yeah, that's also what the Buddha teaches, yet it's completely earth and sky, opposite things.

892
01:16:15,921 --> 01:16:19,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So the packaging sounds the same, inside, completely different thing.

893
01:16:22,042 --> 01:16:25,643
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're judging things only on account of the packaging, you're never going to find this out.

894
01:16:34,763 --> 01:16:40,428
[SPEAKER_03]: I have a follow-up question to the earlier one I had.

895
01:16:40,588 --> 01:16:47,193
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if I... boredom is one of the things that is challenging for me.

896
01:16:47,253 --> 01:16:48,334
[SPEAKER_03]: It's...

897
01:16:49,695 --> 01:16:54,359
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm a very active person and I feel that there is something I could learn from it.

898
01:16:56,140 --> 01:16:59,223
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's quite easy to decide...

899
01:16:59,243 --> 01:17:02,145
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, not easy, but at least doable.

900
01:17:02,820 --> 01:17:14,969
[SPEAKER_03]: To decide, I will not talk, I will not walk, I will not actively do things, I will not watch TV, I will not go for a walk, I'll just sit here.

901
01:17:15,409 --> 01:17:17,871
[SPEAKER_03]: This is not trivial, but easy.

902
01:17:18,451 --> 01:17:20,473
[SPEAKER_03]: But how do you deal with your mind?

903
01:17:20,753 --> 01:17:25,756
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I find it very easy to distract myself with my mind alone.

904
01:17:25,776 --> 01:17:29,619
[SPEAKER_03]: You know, you can start thinking about memories, you can start making plans.

905
01:17:29,779 --> 01:17:31,701
[SPEAKER_03]: Plans are one of the big...

906
01:17:33,234 --> 01:17:39,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, practically, maybe you don't have to do it on such a rigid level to begin with.

907
01:17:40,759 --> 01:17:42,461
[SPEAKER_00]: I will not walk, I will not move.

908
01:17:43,301 --> 01:17:49,205
[SPEAKER_00]: But instead, choose on the level that you can sustain for a longer period of time.

909
01:17:50,526 --> 01:17:58,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Such as, I won't switch the TV on, look at my phone, leave the apartment, but I'll move around, drink tea, sit about.

910
01:17:58,752 --> 01:18:02,075
[SPEAKER_00]: I won't sleep, I won't read, I won't distract myself.

911
01:18:02,914 --> 01:18:03,734
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'll stay here.

912
01:18:04,015 --> 01:18:15,701
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the mind will obviously start thinking about various things, initially, but spend three or four hours, maybe longer, like that, the mind will stop, and then you will be back to where you are.

913
01:18:16,620 --> 01:18:28,943
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you start rigidly, and then you immediately also want to fix the mind to not think about things within that limited framework, because you can't sustain not moving and sitting and not going out and not doing anything for too long.

914
01:18:29,624 --> 01:18:34,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So, find something you can sustain, so that mind can calm down within that, and that's it.

915
01:18:34,265 --> 01:18:34,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you'll see it.

916
01:18:36,045 --> 01:18:39,206
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, to go more gradually about it and not pull in.

917
01:18:39,226 --> 01:18:40,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, exactly, yeah.

918
01:18:40,366 --> 01:18:42,067
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's no problem if you're moving about

919
01:18:43,063 --> 01:18:48,705
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is when you're doing things on account of restlessness, on account of being bored, and so on.

920
01:18:51,847 --> 01:18:57,549
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, walking around back and forth in your apartment can provide you only so much entertainment initially.

921
01:18:58,309 --> 01:19:00,010
[SPEAKER_00]: After, very quickly, you'll be bored with it.

922
01:19:00,210 --> 01:19:00,910
[SPEAKER_00]: So, keep doing it.

923
01:19:02,291 --> 01:19:05,552
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why these repetitive actions, like walking meditation, is good.

924
01:19:06,853 --> 01:19:09,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, oh, I don't want to walk because I'm restless.

925
01:19:09,474 --> 01:19:10,035
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, walk.

926
01:19:10,175 --> 01:19:14,917
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be too distracted by walking up and down the same lane.

927
01:19:15,077 --> 01:19:16,498
[SPEAKER_03]: I can go for...

928
01:19:18,201 --> 01:19:28,129
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, if that's what you're actively doing, then you can't say you're not doing anything.

929
01:19:28,189 --> 01:19:29,490
[SPEAKER_00]: You're actively making plans.

930
01:19:29,830 --> 01:19:30,811
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, don't do that.

931
01:19:31,411 --> 01:19:35,514
[SPEAKER_00]: But if the mind still keeps popping up with things, that's fine.

932
01:19:35,534 --> 01:19:36,415
[SPEAKER_00]: That will calm down.

933
01:19:36,455 --> 01:19:38,857
[SPEAKER_00]: But you also have to stop fueling it.

934
01:19:38,937 --> 01:19:39,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, I'll think about this.

935
01:19:39,838 --> 01:19:40,398
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll think about this.

936
01:19:40,458 --> 01:19:41,099
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll think about this.

937
01:19:41,219 --> 01:19:42,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you do have that choice.

938
01:19:45,550 --> 01:19:51,712
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just, like, drop the ball.

939
01:19:51,792 --> 01:19:57,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, when your mind throws you an idea, you don't hold on to it and you say, okay, I'm not pursuing this.

940
01:19:57,714 --> 01:20:02,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, when the mind throws you the idea, you don't delight in it, you don't welcome it, but you don't deny it either.

941
01:20:03,719 --> 01:20:08,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, see, the people don't want to stay in that kind of state of in-between.

942
01:20:08,881 --> 01:20:12,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind pops up with stuff and I'm enduring, not giving in.

943
01:20:12,963 --> 01:20:15,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they want to prevent the mind from popping up with stuff.

944
01:20:16,164 --> 01:20:19,006
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's usually meditation techniques come for that purpose.

945
01:20:19,086 --> 01:20:21,587
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I can stop thinking because thinking is the problem.

946
01:20:21,607 --> 01:20:21,787
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

947
01:20:22,487 --> 01:20:24,528
[SPEAKER_00]: It's you being moved by the thinking.

948
01:20:24,628 --> 01:20:26,189
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why there is a problem.

949
01:20:26,329 --> 01:20:26,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the thoughts.

950
01:20:28,360 --> 01:20:34,423
[SPEAKER_00]: But for as long as you think the thoughts are the problem, you're mistaking the symptom for the root, for the root cause.

951
01:20:35,784 --> 01:20:41,887
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't try to prevent your mind from coming up with this stuff, but certainly prevent yourself from accepting it.

952
01:20:42,807 --> 01:20:44,508
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the work, that's the endurance.

953
01:20:46,915 --> 01:20:53,539
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't need to endure self-inflicted torture and hunger and sleeping on the nails and whatever else to develop your mind.

954
01:20:53,839 --> 01:20:58,161
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to endure these very things that you have on your day-to-day basis.

955
01:20:58,822 --> 01:21:06,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind throwing stuff at you and you're just blindly catching it and accepting it and following it along.

956
01:21:06,366 --> 01:21:08,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't do that and you will be training properly.

957
01:21:11,349 --> 01:21:12,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, thank you.

958
01:21:14,550 --> 01:21:15,251
[SPEAKER_02]: I would just have a

959
01:21:16,277 --> 01:21:18,678
[SPEAKER_02]: Short question on the daily practice.

960
01:21:19,458 --> 01:21:24,420
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's just the feeling I got from some of the things that you said.

961
01:21:25,160 --> 01:21:41,966
[SPEAKER_02]: Would it be correct to say that you were not particularly keen on, say, suggesting to anyone who started being involved with Buddhism to have like this daily meditation routine?

962
01:21:42,766 --> 01:21:45,187
[SPEAKER_02]: I got the impression that you would

963
01:21:46,415 --> 01:21:51,278
[SPEAKER_02]: You can have a meditation routine.

964
01:21:51,578 --> 01:21:55,740
[SPEAKER_00]: I would just not agree to the type of meditation people are doing.

965
01:21:55,900 --> 01:21:59,901
[SPEAKER_00]: I wouldn't agree to what they call meditation to be.

966
01:22:00,222 --> 01:22:08,285
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, you can... like, routines are useful, certainly in the beginning, but it's like what you perceive to be your duty.

967
01:22:08,545 --> 01:22:09,566
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the problem is.

968
01:22:11,320 --> 01:22:12,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the thing.

969
01:22:12,200 --> 01:22:14,801
[SPEAKER_00]: See, people, I must meditate, you know, because of this, because of that.

970
01:22:15,362 --> 01:22:19,463
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that is rooted in them still not knowing why they're doing it in the first place.

971
01:22:19,803 --> 01:22:21,064
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that should be a meditation.

972
01:22:22,284 --> 01:22:24,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing the intentions behind your actions.

973
01:22:25,526 --> 01:22:29,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Developing the context.

974
01:22:29,687 --> 01:22:38,191
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the reason why I sometimes bring up this question about reflection and dispersivity and whatnot is because, for me personally,

975
01:22:39,181 --> 01:22:56,451
[SPEAKER_02]: It was extremely liberating, where I, at a certain point, allowed myself to actually bring more reflective and discursive activity into my meditation, because it somehow seemed that this is like forbidden.

976
01:22:56,491 --> 01:22:57,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

977
01:22:57,211 --> 01:23:08,137
[SPEAKER_02]: But when at a certain point, for instance, I can give a concrete example, when a very unpleasant feeling arose during meditation,

978
01:23:09,036 --> 01:23:26,423
[SPEAKER_02]: Instead of my trying to either focus on it or focus on the breath, I started asking questions such as, what would it mean for you if this feeling persisted for the rest of your life?

979
01:23:26,743 --> 01:23:28,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's it.

980
01:23:29,724 --> 01:23:31,545
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was the actual meditation part.

981
01:23:33,286 --> 01:23:36,587
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you broaden the context with a simple question aimed rightly.

982
01:23:37,467 --> 01:23:39,187
[SPEAKER_00]: And, by the way, the suttas are full of that.

983
01:23:39,387 --> 01:23:45,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, Maha Moggallāna, before he developed jhāna, the meditation of meditation, how he developed it?

984
01:23:46,449 --> 01:23:49,809
[SPEAKER_00]: By observing the soles of his feet, or nostrils, or... No.

985
01:23:50,269 --> 01:23:55,430
[SPEAKER_00]: He developed it by sitting down and saying, first jhāna, first jhāna, what is the first jhāna?

986
01:23:56,010 --> 01:23:58,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the Buddha said, it's this, this, this, and this.

987
01:23:59,191 --> 01:24:02,011
[SPEAKER_00]: How about I think about all those things and fully understand them?

988
01:24:02,351 --> 01:24:05,152
[SPEAKER_00]: And by doing so, he entered and abided in first jhāna.

989
01:24:06,715 --> 01:24:07,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking and pondering.

990
01:24:08,857 --> 01:24:13,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Basis for arahantship as well.

991
01:24:13,460 --> 01:24:19,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, today, perception is focus, concentrate, ignore, cover up, shut down.

992
01:24:23,647 --> 01:24:24,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, precisely.

993
01:24:24,488 --> 01:24:31,753
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think that this is... it's really important to kind of dispel this notion, this idea.

994
01:24:32,593 --> 01:24:34,795
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, for me, it was...

995
01:24:35,824 --> 01:24:40,908
[SPEAKER_02]: Every now and again, you would have, in different fields or spheres of your life, these qualitative shifts.

996
01:24:41,288 --> 01:24:47,332
[SPEAKER_00]: For me, it was something this simple, that was like an enormous... No, I know, because, you know, like, I started also as a layman.

997
01:24:47,853 --> 01:24:50,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, really, just any form of Buddhism.

998
01:24:50,795 --> 01:24:52,636
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know right from wrong, in that sense.

999
01:24:54,238 --> 01:24:59,942
[SPEAKER_00]: But in hindsight, I could tell then, what things were along the right lines, and which one weren't.

1000
01:25:00,666 --> 01:25:07,032
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was very dutiful when it came to the meditation routine, out in the morning, out in the evening.

1001
01:25:07,252 --> 01:25:11,076
[SPEAKER_00]: It didn't matter every day if I had to work or whatever else.

1002
01:25:11,936 --> 01:25:13,438
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thought that was my duty.

1003
01:25:14,118 --> 01:25:21,425
[SPEAKER_00]: But in hindsight, I realized I would have been better off if I didn't do that and did other things that I didn't know were my duty, but fortunately did them.

1004
01:25:22,005 --> 01:25:24,588
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for example, like I would

1005
01:25:25,986 --> 01:25:31,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Fulfill my duty of meditating for an hour in the morning, in the evening, and then go out with friends.

1006
01:25:32,574 --> 01:25:38,222
[SPEAKER_00]: I would still keep the precepts, that was not the issue, but it would be a fun time.

1007
01:25:40,284 --> 01:25:43,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Men, women, company, all of that.

1008
01:25:44,453 --> 01:25:50,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And I felt fully justified in doing so because I fulfilled my duty of meditation and, hey, I'm still keeping the precepts.

1009
01:25:51,375 --> 01:25:58,616
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in hindsight, I realized I would have been better off if I didn't spend two hours watching the nostrils and if I didn't go out with the friends.

1010
01:26:00,137 --> 01:26:00,497
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

1011
01:26:01,217 --> 01:26:08,299
[SPEAKER_00]: And all those times when I didn't go out like that for the sake of entertainment or distraction, that's why I could then see later on in life.

1012
01:26:08,339 --> 01:26:09,999
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where I benefited from.

1013
01:26:10,259 --> 01:26:12,920
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that I knew it, but I kept doing it because it felt right.

1014
01:26:15,538 --> 01:26:17,740
[SPEAKER_00]: It felt like giving to the weakness otherwise.

1015
01:26:18,500 --> 01:26:21,042
[SPEAKER_00]: But nobody told me that that was right thing to do.

1016
01:26:21,863 --> 01:26:29,128
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of okay, you assume, but no, the right thing is to watch your nostrils and observe your movements and touching, touching, feeling, feeling, sitting, sitting and everything else.

1017
01:26:30,469 --> 01:26:34,492
[SPEAKER_00]: And you take that as a duty and it's completely misguided, utterly.

1018
01:26:36,813 --> 01:26:38,855
[SPEAKER_00]: But if somebody told me, no, don't do any of that.

1019
01:26:39,675 --> 01:26:42,618
[SPEAKER_00]: But every single day of your life,

1020
01:26:43,528 --> 01:26:50,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Be aware for the motivation behind your actions and don't act towards lust, aversion and distraction.

1021
01:26:51,753 --> 01:26:52,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter what it is.

1022
01:26:55,435 --> 01:26:57,016
[SPEAKER_00]: It would have saved me so much time as well.

1023
01:27:01,299 --> 01:27:04,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if you want to sit quietly and think about wholesome things, that's fine.

1024
01:27:06,782 --> 01:27:08,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And make a routine out of it, that's fine.

1025
01:27:10,457 --> 01:27:13,019
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's where the benefit is and that's where the work is.

1026
01:27:13,719 --> 01:27:15,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but I fulfill my duty.

1027
01:27:15,600 --> 01:27:19,282
[SPEAKER_00]: I can go out, you know, I can, I can, I can enjoy this, I can enjoy it.

1028
01:27:19,362 --> 01:27:22,104
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, but that's why you're not progressing in the practice.

1029
01:27:25,686 --> 01:27:28,548
[SPEAKER_00]: And you realize, okay, so that's where the emphasis should be.

1030
01:27:29,409 --> 01:27:35,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Not on this misguided duty of observance of sensations of your body or something, that you won't find in the suttas.

1031
01:27:35,973 --> 01:27:36,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way.

1032
01:27:40,650 --> 01:27:55,843
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, this resonates very well with me because it was... it's almost as if... there's this idea almost as if meditation was some sort of a syrup that you take.

1033
01:27:56,163 --> 01:27:56,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1034
01:27:56,403 --> 01:27:58,345
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, it kind of does its own thing.

1035
01:27:58,465 --> 01:27:59,866
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like a mechanism.

1036
01:27:59,986 --> 01:28:00,847
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

1037
01:28:00,887 --> 01:28:03,249
[SPEAKER_00]: You set it in motion and I don't know what's going on.

1038
01:28:03,289 --> 01:28:03,469
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1039
01:28:03,889 --> 01:28:05,311
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you just get these results.

1040
01:28:05,471 --> 01:28:06,452
[SPEAKER_02]: It just does the thing.

1041
01:28:06,512 --> 01:28:06,712
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

1042
01:28:11,801 --> 01:28:12,362
Yes, okay.

1043
01:28:12,783 --> 01:28:13,404
Thank you very much.

1044
01:28:13,484 --> 01:28:16,911
A lot of things to be said here, but maybe we save some for next time.

